#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""
perry-goals — the deterministic read of a project's goals, and its writer.

DESIGN-005 steps 2 and 3. The third of Perry's read contracts, after
`perry-task/list/*` and `perry-decide/list/*`, and the last one needed before a
front-end can show a whole project without opening a single markdown file.

**It parses nothing itself.** Every field below comes from
`viewer/parsers.py § load_snapshot`, the same code `perry-state` and the viewer
use. That is not laziness — a second parser of one file is the defect this
project has now hit twice: `perry-task` placed board cells by resolved header
name while `viewer/parsers.py` read them by position, and on a board with one
extra column every task's owner was reported as its track with the linter
calling it clean. A fourth reader of `OKR.md` would be a fourth chance at the
same shape. This tool composes and reshapes; it does not read.

What it adds over `perry-state --json`, which already carries the nested
structures: a **flat `krs` array**. A front-end wants "every KR, with where it
sits and how far along it is" — walking objectives→krs and re-deriving progress
is exactly the estimating `schema/README.md` forbids, done in a repo Perry's
tests cannot reach.

## The write side (TASK-037 / TASK-042)

`commit` is the first write path, because `OKR.md § Commitments` is the one
section with a live consumer and no writer at all: `modes/pipeline.md` and
`modes/queue.md` both name it as their spine, and until now the procedure was
prose in `goals/reference/phases.md § commit <promise>` that an agent followed
by hand. That page is this tool's acceptance criteria, rule for rule.

**`OKR.md` is never re-rendered.** `Okr` below edits lines in place, and cells
in place inside those lines — see `splice_cell`. A writer that round-trips
prose through a parsed model produces a file that still passes `perry-lint` and
no longer reads the way its author wrote it, which DESIGN-005 § 5.5 rates worse
than having no writer at all.

**`okr.jsonl` is the truth for the register, and `OKR.md § Commitments` is the
projection of it** (ADR-007 decision 2, TASK-123). `commit` reads that store
BEFORE it decides anything, and a cell the two disagree about is a hand edit it
refuses to act on — `register_drift` and `check_register_drift`. It does not
re-render the file to get there; it compares, and once the comparison passes,
every cell read out of the markdown below is provably the store's own value.
`--accept-hand-edit` is the way past, with the meaning it already has here: the
FILE's value becomes the new truth and the next write records it.

**The log is the truth, the file is the view** — accepted by the user on
2026-08-17 (DESIGN-005 § 9, last entry) *before* this writer was built, exactly
so it would not be built against "the file is the truth". Every write appends
an event to `.perry/events.jsonl`, and a row the tool wrote whose cells no
longer match the log is a **hand edit**: reported and reconciled with the
user's value, never overwritten with the log's.

**This tool writes `OKR.md` and `phase/` and nothing else.** `BOARD.md`,
`journal/` and `DECISIONS.md` belong to other lanes, and `SKILL.md § The
hand-off contract` names all three as cases that must refuse. `assert_owned`
is that rule as code, and the board-side link — a `Commitment` cell carrying an
`Id` from this table — is printed as a hand-off for `/perry work` to write.

Usage:
  perry-goals list [--json] [--level overall|phase]

  perry-goals commit --track <t> --promise <text> --to <whom> --due <when>
                     [--by-when-note <prose>]
  perry-goals commit --id <Id> [--due <when>] [--by-when-note <prose>]
                                [--promise <t>] [--to <whom>]
                                [--discharged-by <text>]
  perry-goals commit --close <Id> [--discharged-by <text>]
  perry-goals commit --miss <Id> --reason <text>
  perry-goals commit --migrate       split a pre-TASK-091 `By when` column

  perry-goals link <TASK-ID> <KR-ID>
  perry-goals link --alias <PROJECT-ID> "<the other name>"
  perry-goals link --unlinked <TASK-ID>
  perry-goals link --project <PROJECT-ID> <KR-ID> "<name>"

  `link` writes `phase/<NNN>-linkage.md` for the CURRENT phase, in place.
  <KR-ID> may also be a Project id or a registered alias, resolved in that
  order; anything that does not resolve to exactly ONE KR is REFUSED with its
  candidates named. Nothing here is fuzzy-matched, and nothing here writes a
  `target` or a `current` — see `goals/reference/linkage.md`.

  --due <when>    TYPED: an ISO date (2026-09-30) or an SLA token (3d, 2w).
                  Anything else is refused, in every language.
  --by-when-note <prose>
                  PROSE: how the deadline was worded to the party it was
                  promised to. **Never validated.** No regex asks it anything.

  --root <path>   project root; default $PERRY_PROJECT, else walk up from cwd
  --json          machine-readable result
  --dry-run       print the plan; write nothing
  --accept-hand-edit
                  proceed over a row the log and the file disagree about,
                  taking the FILE's value as the new truth

Exit codes:
  0  read, or written
  1  refused — the reason is printed, and nothing was written
  2  bad invocation

No LLM, no external dependencies (stdlib only). DESIGN-005.
"""

from __future__ import annotations

import json
import os
import sys
import re
from contextlib import contextmanager
from dataclasses import asdict, is_dataclass
from datetime import date, datetime
from pathlib import Path

HERE = Path(__file__).resolve().parent
PERRY_HOME = Path(os.environ.get("PERRY_HOME") or HERE.parent).resolve()
SCHEMA_PATH = PERRY_HOME / "schema" / "state-schema.json"
TEMPLATE_PATH = PERRY_HOME / "goals" / "state" / "OKR_TEMPLATE.md"

sys.path.insert(0, str(PERRY_HOME / "viewer"))
sys.path.insert(0, str(PERRY_HOME / "bin"))
import parsers as P  # noqa: E402
from tables import (UnrenderableCell, append_cell, cell_spans,  # noqa: E402
                    render_row, splice_cell, split_row, squash)
import lib  # noqa: E402
# TASK-092. The store `OKR.md` is now a projection of, and its renderer. This
# tool does not carry a second copy of either, for the same reason it parses
# nothing itself: a fourth reader of `OKR.md` would be a fourth chance at the
# defect that made `perry-task` and `viewer/parsers.py` disagree in silence.
import perry_md_store as md_store  # noqa: E402


class Refused(Exception):
    """A refusal is a first-class outcome, not a crash. Nothing was written."""


@contextmanager
def naming_the_flag(flag: "str | list[str]"):
    """Attach the flag a value arrived on to any `UnrenderableCell` inside.

    **`UnrenderableCell` carries `index`, which is a COLUMN POSITION**, and a
    column position means nothing to the person who typed the command:
    `--promise` and `--to` produced byte-identical refusals and neither said
    what to fix. The rule this restores is written over `SLA_TOKEN_RE` —
    *"the refusal says so and names the flag, so a user is never guessing
    where their words go"* — and `cmd_commit`'s whitespace refusal obeyed it
    (`--promise was given only whitespace…`) while its line-break sibling,
    on the same tool and the same value, did not.

    The flag is known only at the write site, which is why it is attached
    here rather than guessed later from the value: `bin/perry-task` matches
    `exc.value` back against `args` at its boundary, and that match is exact
    or nothing, so two flags carrying the same string name whichever comes
    first in a hand-kept list. A write site knows without matching.

    `flag` is one flag when the write is one cell, or a list parallel to the
    row's cells when the write is a whole row.
    """
    try:
        yield
    except UnrenderableCell as exc:
        if isinstance(flag, str):
            exc.naming(flag)
        elif 0 <= exc.index < len(flag):
            exc.naming(flag[exc.index])
        raise


def unrenderable_refusal(exc: UnrenderableCell) -> str:
    """`UnrenderableCell` as the one sentence the refusal channel prints.

    One spelling, used by `main` (which knows `--json`) and by the
    module-level last resort (which does not). Two spellings of one rule is
    the defect `tests/test_one_line_break_rule.py` was opened for —
    `render_row` and `check_cell` each carried their own line-break test and
    disagreed on six of the eleven boundaries `str.splitlines()` breaks on.
    """
    where = f"{exc.field} " if exc.field else ""
    return (f"{where}was given {exc.value[:60]!r}, which {exc.why}. "
            f"A register cell is one line of a markdown table.")


_SCHEMA = None


def load_schema() -> dict:
    """`lib.load_schema`, memoized. The memo is this tool's, not the library's.

    `okr_table_spec` and its callers read the schema on nearly every code path,
    and caching it in the shared function would hand every other tool a cache
    it never asked for — including the ones a test re-execs in-process."""
    global _SCHEMA
    if _SCHEMA is None:
        _SCHEMA = lib.load_schema(Refused)
    return _SCHEMA


def okr_table_spec(under: str) -> dict:
    """The schema's own declaration of one `OKR.md` table.

    Read rather than restated. The Commitments table's heading regex, its six
    columns and its one optional column are all declared in
    `schema/state-schema.json`, and a writer that hardcoded a second copy of
    them would be the "one rule, two implementations" defect this project has
    now found in five review rounds — with the added twist that `perry-lint`
    validates against the schema's copy, so the two would disagree silently
    until a project used a spelling only one of them knew.
    """
    for spec in load_schema()["files"]:
        if spec.get("path") != "OKR.md":
            continue
        for table in spec.get("tables", []):
            if table.get("under") == under:
                return table
    raise Refused(f"schema/state-schema.json declares no OKR.md table under "
                  f"{under!r}; this tool cannot invent one")


#: `^Commitments\b|^承诺` at the time of writing — taken from the schema so the
#: heading this tool finds and the heading `perry-lint` validates are one
#: string.
COMMITMENTS_UNDER = "^Commitments\\b|^承诺"


def heading_pattern(under: str) -> str:
    """A schema `under` regex, as a pattern that matches *after* the hashes.

    The schema anchors every alternative with `^` because `perry-lint` applies
    it to a heading line whose `## ` has already been stripped. `Okr.section`
    applies it after `^##\\s+`, where a second `^` can never match — so a
    literal hand-off of the schema's string silently finds no section at all,
    and the tool reports a missing `## Commitments` on a file that has one.
    Dropping the anchors is the entire translation; the alternatives stay the
    schema's, which is the point of reading them from there.
    """
    return re.sub(r"(?:^|(?<=\|))\^", "", under)


COMMITMENTS_HEADING = heading_pattern(COMMITMENTS_UNDER)


def column_spellings(canonical: str) -> list[str]:
    """Every squashed spelling of one column name, English first.

    Resolution here is deliberately **table-local**: "which header cell of THIS
    table is `By when`", not "what does this header cell mean anywhere". The
    global direction is ambiguous and the ambiguity is real — `schema/
    state-schema.json § i18n.columns` maps both `Deadline` and `By when` onto
    the same Chinese word `截止`, because a KR table's deadline and a
    commitment's due date are the same word in Chinese and different columns in
    Perry. Asked globally, `截止` resolves to `Deadline` and a Chinese
    Commitments table loses its `By when` column. Asked table-locally, the
    question has one answer.
    """
    i18n = load_schema().get("i18n", {}).get("columns", {}) or {}
    out = [squash(canonical)]
    for lang_spellings in (i18n.get(canonical) or {}).values():
        out += [squash(s) for s in lang_spellings]
    return out


def column_at(header: list[str], canonical: str) -> int:
    """Index of `canonical` in `header`, or -1. By NAME, never by position."""
    want = set(column_spellings(canonical))
    for i, cell in enumerate(header):
        if squash(cell) in want:
            return i
    return -1


def header_language(header: list[str], declared: list[str]) -> str:
    """Which language THIS table's headers are written in, `en` by default.

    Table-local like everything else here, and for a further reason: a file may
    hold an English KR table and a Chinese Commitments table, because the two
    were written at different times by different hands. Asking the document is
    asking the wrong scope.
    """
    i18n = load_schema().get("i18n", {}).get("columns", {}) or {}
    cells = {squash(c) for c in header}
    for canonical in declared:
        for lang, spellings in (i18n.get(canonical) or {}).items():
            if cells & {squash(s) for s in spellings}:
                return lang
    return "en"


def display_name(canonical: str, lang: str) -> str:
    """How to WRITE a column name into a table in `lang`.

    A widened Chinese table gets a Chinese header. Writing `Discharged by` into
    `| 编号 | 轨道 | … |` produces a table this tool can still read and a human
    cannot, which is the same failure as reformatting prose — the file stops
    being the user's.
    """
    spellings = (load_schema().get("i18n", {}).get("columns", {})
                 .get(canonical) or {}).get(lang) or []
    return spellings[0] if spellings else canonical


# ── one cell, without re-spacing the rest of its row ───────────────────────


SEPARATOR_RE = re.compile(r"^\s*\|[\s:|-]+\|?\s*$")


def append_separator_cell(line: str) -> str:
    """One more column on a table's separator row, in that row's own style.

    `|---|---|` and `|-------|:-----:|` are both real and both in this repo.
    Copying the last cell keeps whichever one the file uses instead of
    imposing a third.
    """
    spans = cell_spans(line)
    last = line[spans[-1][0]:spans[-1][1]] if spans else " --- "
    body = line.rstrip()
    # **A separator row need not end in `|` either.** This assumed one where
    # `append_cell` explicitly handles its absence, so widening a table written
    # without trailing pipes produced a 7-cell header, a 6-cell separator and
    # 7-cell rows — rc 0, no warning, and `perry-lint` silent. The two halves
    # of one widen have to make the same assumption or the widen is not atomic.
    if not body.endswith("|") or body.endswith("\\|"):
        return body + "|" + last + "|"
    return body + last + "|"


class Okr:
    """`OKR.md` as a list of lines, edited in place. Never re-rendered.

    The same rule `perry-task`'s `Board` states, and it binds harder here:
    `BOARD.md` is mostly table, `OKR.md` is mostly prose the user argued with.
    A writer that round-trips through a parsed model normalizes whitespace,
    heading punctuation and blank-line runs, and the result still passes
    `perry-lint` while no longer reading the way its author wrote it — the one
    failure DESIGN-005 § 5.5 says is worse than having no writer at all.

    So the gate this class exists to pass, before any write path ships:

        load it, change nothing, write it back  →  byte-identical

    over every real `OKR.md` available, not over a fixture Perry generated.
    The four on this machine disagree about almost everything — heading
    punctuation, language, whether `Anti-Goals` sits inside a version, whether
    version numbers are contiguous — and not one of them is malformed.
    """

    def __init__(self, path: Path):
        self.path = path
        if not path.exists():
            raise Refused(f"no OKR.md at {path}")
        self.text = path.read_text()
        self.lines = self.text.split("\n")

    def render(self) -> str:
        """Exactly what `__init__` read, unless a cell was changed.

        `split("\n")` / `join("\n")` is lossless in both directions,
        including for a file with no trailing newline and for one with
        several — which `splitlines()` is not, and which is why it is not
        used.
        """
        return "\n".join(self.lines)

    def unchanged(self) -> bool:
        return self.render() == self.text

    # -- locating

    def section(self, pattern: str, level: int = 2) -> tuple[int, int]:
        """(first line, end line) of the first heading matching `pattern`.

        `end` is the next heading at the same level **or shallower**, so a
        `## Commitments` section ends at the next `##` or `#` and swallows any
        `###` beneath it. gimegime-pmo nests `### Anti-Goals` inside a version
        section; a scanner that stopped at any heading would cut that version
        in half.
        """
        hashes = "#" * level
        rx = re.compile(rf"^{hashes}\s+(?:{pattern})", re.I)
        stop = re.compile(rf"^#{{1,{level}}}\s")
        start = None
        for i, line in enumerate(self.lines):
            if start is None:
                if rx.match(line):
                    start = i
            elif stop.match(line):
                return start, i
        if start is None:
            raise Refused(
                f"OKR.md has no `{hashes} {pattern}` section")
        return start, len(self.lines)

    def has_section(self, pattern: str, level: int = 2) -> bool:
        try:
            self.section(pattern, level)
            return True
        except Refused:
            return False

    def table(self, lo: int, hi: int) -> tuple[int, list[str]]:
        """(separator line index, header cells) for the first table in range.

        The header is the line above the separator, which is how a markdown
        table is actually defined — looking for a line of pipes and calling
        the first one a header would match a row.
        """
        for i in range(lo, hi):
            if re.match(r"^\|\s*:?-{2,}", self.lines[i]) and i > lo:
                return i, split_row(self.lines[i - 1])
        raise Refused("that section has no table")

    def rows(self, lo: int, hi: int) -> list[tuple[int, dict]]:
        """(line index, cells keyed by squashed header) for each data row."""
        sep, header = self.table(lo, hi)
        keys = [squash(h) for h in header]
        out = []
        for i in range(sep + 1, hi):
            line = self.lines[i]
            if not line.strip().startswith("|"):
                if line.strip():
                    break
                continue
            out.append((i, dict(zip(keys, split_row(line)))))
        return out

    def row_indexes(self, lo: int, hi: int) -> list[int]:
        """Just the line numbers of the data rows, in file order."""
        return [i for i, _ in self.rows(lo, hi)]

    # -- editing

    def set_cell(self, index: int, column: int, value: str) -> None:
        """One cell of one row. Every other cell keeps its own bytes."""
        self.lines[index] = splice_cell(self.lines[index], column, value)

    def widen(self, lo: int, hi: int, name: str) -> list[str]:
        """Add one column to a section's table, and pad every row for it.

        The only edit here that touches lines the caller did not name, which is
        why it is reported to the user rather than done quietly. It is still
        the right answer: `Discharged by` is optional in the schema, `--miss`
        has nowhere to put its reason without it, and refusing would strand a
        real promise on a table that is not malformed.
        """
        sep, header = self.table(lo, hi)
        rows = self.row_indexes(lo, hi)
        self.lines[sep - 1] = append_cell(self.lines[sep - 1], name)
        self.lines[sep] = append_separator_cell(self.lines[sep])
        for i in rows:
            self.lines[i] = append_cell(self.lines[i], "")
        return header + [name]

    def append_table_row(self, lo: int, hi: int, cells: list[str]) -> tuple[int, str]:
        """Insert a rendered row after the table's last row. (index, line)."""
        sep, _ = self.table(lo, hi)
        rows = self.row_indexes(lo, hi)
        at = (rows[-1] if rows else sep) + 1
        line = render_row(cells)
        self.lines.insert(at, line)
        return at, line

    def insert_section(self, block: list[str], before: list[str]) -> int:
        """Insert a `## ` section before the first heading in `before`.

        Falls back to the end of the file. Placement is by the file's own
        headings rather than by line number, because the four real `OKR.md`
        files on this machine agree about almost nothing else — one nests
        `### Anti-Goals` inside a version, one carries a section Perry's
        template has never heard of, and one has versions `v2` and `v4` with no
        `v3`. Appending at a computed offset would land inside a version block
        on at least one of them.
        """
        at = None
        for pattern in before:
            if self.has_section(pattern):
                at = self.section(pattern)[0]
                break
        if at is None:
            at = len(self.lines)
            # Land after the file's last non-blank line, keeping however many
            # trailing newlines it had — `render` is byte-exact and a section
            # appended past them would move them.
            while at > 0 and not self.lines[at - 1].strip():
                at -= 1
            self.lines[at:at] = [""] + block
            return at + 1
        self.lines[at:at] = block + [""]
        return at


# Versioned independently of the other two (DESIGN-005 § 4 decision 5). Goals
# move on a different clock from tasks; tying the versions together would make
# a consumer re-check code for a change in a domain it does not read.
#
# TASK-037 shipped the writer and did NOT move this. See
# `schema/goals-list-contract.md § Changelog`: a writer is not a read-contract
# change, and the commitments register gained no key here.
#
# TASK-120 DOES move it, to `2.1`. Four keys are added and none is removed or
# retyped: `krs[].current_provenance`, `krs[].current_staleness`,
# `krs[].linked_task_completion` and `conformance.krs_with_stale_current`. The
# contract document's own rule for this is written at the bottom of its
# Changelog — "if it is added later it is `2.1`, additive, and this table
# records it" — so the minor moves in the same edit that adds the keys.
#
# TASK-144 moves it to `2.2`, and adds no key at all. The three timestamps in
# those blocks — `asserted_at`, `staleness.since` and `moved_tasks[].at` — are
# UTC now and carry a `Z`, where `at` used to be the log's LOCAL text and was
# compared against the register's UTC as though the two were one clock. Same
# keys, different values, which is exactly the case `perry-task/events/1.1`
# moved its own minor for. A consumer rendering `at` sees the change.
#
# TASK-205 moves it to `2.3`, and the move is a plain key addition: this
# payload now carries `semantics`, the array below. Nothing already emitted
# changed, so `LIST_SEMANTICS` gains no entry for `2.3` itself —
# `perry-events/list/1.1` added the same key under the same reading.
LIST_CONTRACT = "perry-goals/list/2.3"

# **Minors where a value's MEANING changed, not just where a key was added.**
#
# The same rule, shape and order as `bin/perry-task § LIST_SEMANTICS`, and the
# same reason: rule 2 promises `2.x` only adds keys, that promise is unbroken,
# and it does not cover a value that starts returning something different
# under a key that did not move. A consumer records the minor it read against
# and reports every entry newer than that, which is why the list is **ordered
# oldest first** — a slice, not a search. Append; never insert where it reads
# well. `bin/perry-task` shipped 1.5, 1.9, 1.7 once, and a test caught it.
#
# **`2.1` is deliberately not here.** It added four keys and moved no existing
# value. An entry for it would tell a consumer still sitting on `2.0` that a
# field it has never seen changed meaning, which is a false alarm rather than
# a warning — the call `perry-task` made for its own `1.15` and `1.17`, both
# recorded as decisions in `tests/`.
LIST_SEMANTICS = [
    {"version": "2.2",
     "fields": ["krs[].current_provenance.asserted_at",
                "krs[].current_staleness.since",
                "krs[].current_staleness.moved_tasks[].at"],
     "note": "All three are UTC now and carry a `Z`. `at` in particular used "
             "to be the event log's LOCAL text, and it was compared against "
             "the register's UTC as though the two were one clock — so "
             "`current_staleness.stale` answered wrongly inside the machine's "
             "offset, in one direction or the other, on every project not "
             "running at UTC+0. No key was added, removed or retyped; the "
             "same three keys return a different string. A consumer that "
             "parsed these as naive local timestamps, or that compared one of "
             "them against a clock of its own, was correct before 2.2 and is "
             "not now."},
]

#: `list` only reads, and reading is never gated (ADR-004). Every other
#: subcommand writes `OKR.md`, which is the one file this lane gates on — one
#: key, one file, per ADR-004 § 5.
READ_ONLY_COMMANDS = {"list"}
GATED_FILE = "OKR.md"

_PERRY_CONFORM = None
_PERRY_STATE = None


def _sibling(name: str):
    """`bin/<name>` as a module, loaded once. The pattern `perry-task` and
    `perry-decide` already use to reach each other's rules rather than copy
    them."""
    import importlib.machinery
    import importlib.util
    mod_name = name.replace("-", "_")
    sys.path.insert(0, str(PERRY_HOME / "bin"))
    spec = importlib.util.spec_from_loader(
        mod_name, importlib.machinery.SourceFileLoader(
            mod_name, str(PERRY_HOME / "bin" / name)))
    mod = importlib.util.module_from_spec(spec)
    # See the identical note in `bin/perry-task`: `dataclasses` resolves
    # `from __future__ import annotations` strings through `sys.modules`.
    sys.modules[mod_name] = mod
    spec.loader.exec_module(mod)
    return mod


def perry_conform():
    """`bin/perry-conform` as a module — the conformance gate (ADR-004)."""
    global _PERRY_CONFORM
    if _PERRY_CONFORM is None:
        _PERRY_CONFORM = _sibling("perry-conform")
    return _PERRY_CONFORM


def perry_state():
    """`bin/perry-state` as a module — for `parse_tracks` and nothing else.

    The track register lives in `.perry/config.md § Tracks` and one reader of
    it already exists. A second would be a second opinion about which track is
    `queue` mode, and this tool refuses a promise based on that answer.
    """
    global _PERRY_STATE
    if _PERRY_STATE is None:
        _PERRY_STATE = _sibling("perry-state")
    return _PERRY_STATE


# ── the hand-off contract, as code ────────────────────────────────────────

#: Everything the `goals` lane may write, as paths relative to the state root.
#: `SKILL.md § The hand-off contract`. Stated as a predicate over the whole
#: filesystem rather than as a blacklist of the three files that lane keeps
#: getting wrong: the rule is "no lane writes outside its own", and a list of
#: forbidden names would pass a write to `weekly/` or `evidence/` that the same
#: rule forbids for the same reason.
def owned_by_goals(relative: str) -> bool:
    return relative == "OKR.md" or relative.startswith("phase/")


HANDOFF = {
    "BOARD.md": "work", "journal": "work", "PROJECT_STATE.md": "work",
    "evidence": "work", "weekly": "work", "handoff": "work",
    "DECISIONS.md": "decide", "decisions": "decide", "design": "decide",
}


def assert_owned(state_root: Path, path: Path) -> None:
    """Refuse, by name, any write outside the `goals` lane's own files.

    A lane that needs a change in another lane's file **asks in chat and
    stops** — it does not write and apologise. This is that sentence with a
    stack trace attached, so the rule cannot be broken by a future subcommand
    forgetting it.
    """
    try:
        rel = path.resolve().relative_to(state_root.resolve()).as_posix()
    except ValueError:
        rel = str(path)
    if owned_by_goals(rel):
        return
    lane = HANDOFF.get(rel) or HANDOFF.get(rel.split("/", 1)[0]) or "another lane"
    raise Refused(
        f"`goals` does not write {rel} — it belongs to `{lane}` "
        f"(SKILL.md § The hand-off contract). Hand it off: say what needs to "
        f"change and let `/perry {lane}` write it. Nothing was written")


def write_atomic(state_root: Path, path: Path, text: str) -> None:
    """`lib.write_atomic` behind this lane's hand-off gate.

    The extra argument and the extra line are the whole difference, and they
    are not part of the primitive: `assert_owned` is the `goals` lane refusing
    to write another lane's file (`SKILL.md § The hand-off contract`), which
    only this tool has. The atomic write underneath is everyone's."""
    assert_owned(state_root, path)
    lib.write_atomic(path, text)


def write_okr_and_store(state_root: Path, project_root: Path, okr,
                        touched, quiet: bool = False) -> dict:
    """Write `OKR.md` **and** the store it is a projection of — or neither.

    TASK-092. `OKR.md` is still edited in place, cell by cell, because
    DESIGN-005 § 5.5 rates a re-rendered prose file worse than no writer at
    all — that has not changed and must not. What changes is that the typed
    half of the file now also lives in `okr.jsonl` beside it in the state
    root, and this is the one place the two are written together.

    **The byte comparison is the gate, and it is the check that made this row
    dispatchable at all.** `TASK-037-spec` is marked manual on DESIGN-005 §
    5.5's verdict that "the risk is not one a test catches: the failure mode is
    a file that still parses and no longer reads the way its author wrote it".
    So before either file lands, the store is derived from the text this write
    produced, rendered back, and compared byte for byte with it. A file that no
    longer reads the way its author wrote it fails that comparison by
    definition. It is a refusal rather than a warning, and it happens while
    nothing has been written.

    **A hand edit is REPORTED, never refused.** That is one of Perry's own
    Operating Principles, and this function obeys it rather than inventing a
    second policy beside `check_hand_edit`: a store value the file no longer
    agrees with, on a row this command did not touch, comes back in
    `store_drift` and is printed. It is not silently absorbed — that is the
    measured defect `bin/perry-tasks § write` records, a canonical value
    replaced by the projection and reported as a success — and it is not
    refused either. `perry-okr diff` is where the same drift is asked about on
    its own, and `perry-okr render --write` is the way back.

    `touched` names the record-key prefixes this write is allowed to move.
    """
    text = okr.render()
    store = md_store.OKR.store_path(project_root, state_root)
    derived = md_store.derive(md_store.OKR, text)
    out = {"store": str(store), "store_records": len(derived),
           "store_drift": [], "records_the_file_no_longer_renders": []}
    prior: list[dict] = []
    if store.exists():
        try:
            prior, malformed = md_store.validate_records(
                md_store.load_store(store))
        except (OSError, ValueError) as exc:
            raise Refused(
                f"{store} exists but is not readable JSONL "
                f"({type(exc).__name__}: {exc}). Refusing to overwrite a store "
                f"this tool cannot compare against; move it aside and re-run. "
                f"Nothing was written") from None
        if malformed:
            raise Refused(
                f"{store} is malformed at line {malformed[0]['line']} "
                f"({malformed[0]['message']}); refusing to overwrite a store "
                f"that cannot be safely compared. Nothing was written")

    # **A record whose line has left the file is KEPT (TASK-123).** Deriving
    # the whole store from the text meant a row deleted from `OKR.md` by hand
    # deleted the canonical record with it — the file editing the store, which
    # is the direction ADR-007 reverses, and `perry-okr write --from-file`
    # refuses this exact loss by name (*"the whole record would be dropped"*)
    # while this path performed it and reported success. Nothing renders for a
    # kept record, so `rendered == text` still holds below and `perry-okr diff`
    # stays byte-clean; `perry-okr verify` is where it shows, under
    # `records_not_in_the_file`, which is what "reported rather than honoured"
    # means for a row that is gone rather than changed.
    #
    # No write path in this tool removes a line — `commit` appends, amends and
    # widens — so a key that has left is a hand edit by construction.
    have = {md_store.record_key(r) for r in derived}
    kept = [r for r in prior if md_store.record_key(r) not in have]
    final = derived + kept
    out["records_the_file_no_longer_renders"] = sorted(
        md_store.record_key(r).replace("\x00", "/") for r in kept)
    out["store_records"] = len(final)

    _valid, malformed_now = md_store.validate_records(final)
    if malformed_now:
        raise Refused(
            f"the write would produce an OKR store this tool could not read "
            f"back ({malformed_now[0]['message']}). Two lines of `OKR.md` "
            f"carrying one key is the usual cause. Nothing was written")
    rendered, report = md_store.render(md_store.OKR, text, final)
    if rendered != text:
        where = report.get("first_difference") or {}
        raise Refused(
            f"the OKR store cannot reproduce the file this write would "
            f"produce, so writing either would lose the difference between "
            f"them (first at line {where.get('line', '?')}). That is this "
            f"renderer's bug, not the file's. Nothing was written")
    if prior:
        out["store_drift"] = [
            line for line in md_store.would_discard(prior, final)
            if not md_store.touches(line, touched)]
    if out["store_drift"] and not quiet:
        print(f"perry-goals: ⚠ `OKR.md` was edited by hand since the store was "
              f"last written — {len(out['store_drift'])} value(s) on row(s) "
              f"this command does not touch. The file's values are taken as "
              f"the new truth, which is what a hand edit means; "
              f"`perry-okr diff` asks about it on its own.", file=sys.stderr)
        for line in out["store_drift"][:5]:
            print(f"    {line}", file=sys.stderr)
    if out["records_the_file_no_longer_renders"] and not quiet:
        print(f"perry-goals: ⚠ {len(out['records_the_file_no_longer_renders'])} "
              f"record(s) are in `okr.jsonl` and no longer have a row in "
              f"`OKR.md`: "
              f"{', '.join(out['records_the_file_no_longer_renders'][:5])}. "
              f"The store keeps them — a row deleted from a rendered file does "
              f"not delete what it means (ADR-007 decision 2), and their ids "
              f"are never reused. `perry-okr verify` names them; `perry-okr "
              f"render --write` cannot bring a deleted row back, so restoring "
              f"one is a hand edit or a new `commit`.", file=sys.stderr)
    write_atomic(state_root, okr.path, text)
    # The store is written second and unconditionally, under the same project
    # lock as the file above, so a reader cannot catch the pair half-written.
    # If the process dies between the two, the next command reports the
    # difference as drift rather than absorbing it in silence.
    lib.write_atomic(store, md_store.store_text(final))
    return out


def project_lock(state_root: Path, timeout: float = 10.0):
    """The same lock `perry-task` and `perry-decide` take, on the same key.

    One lock per project, not one per lane: a `goals` write and a `work` write
    touch different files but the same project, and a reader that catches them
    mid-flight sees a state neither lane intended (DESIGN-005 § 5.4).
    """
    return lib.project_lock(state_root, timeout, Refused)


# ── events ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# `.perry/events.jsonl` is the project's log, not a lane's file — `perry-task`
# appends to it today and DESIGN-005 § 5.2 makes it canonical for tasks in step
# 4. The user accepted that direction on 2026-08-17, BEFORE this writer existed
# and explicitly so that it would not be built against "the file is the truth"
# (DESIGN-005 § 9, last entry). So every write here emits an event, and the
# reconcile direction below is the one that decision implies.
#
# Event names are prefixed `commit-` and never reuse `add` / `route` / `done` /
# `drop`: `perry-state § reconcile_drift` keys the board's orphan detector on
# those four names, and a commitment id it had never seen carrying one of them
# would be reported as a task whose row went missing.


def events_path(project_root: Path) -> Path:
    return project_root / ".perry" / "events.jsonl"


def read_events(project_root: Path) -> list[dict]:
    p = events_path(project_root)
    if not p.exists():
        return []
    try:
        text = p.read_text(errors="replace")
    except OSError:
        # The log is derived and disposable (DESIGN-004 § 5.3). An unreadable
        # one costs history resolution; it must not be able to stop a write to
        # the canonical file, or the derived record would be load-bearing after
        # all.
        return []
    out = []
    for line in text.split("\n"):
        line = line.strip()
        if not line:
            continue
        try:
            out.append(json.loads(line))
        except json.JSONDecodeError:
            continue  # a corrupt line is not a reason to refuse a write
    return out


COMMIT_EVENTS = ("commit", "commit-update", "commit-close", "commit-miss")


def commitment_events(events: list[dict], cid: str = "") -> list[dict]:
    return [e for e in events if e.get("event") in COMMIT_EVENTS
            and (not cid or e.get("id") == cid)]


def logged_status(events: list[dict], cid: str) -> str | None:
    """What the log says this commitment's `Status` is, or None if it has
    never heard of it.

    None is not "unknown", it is **"predates the tool"** — and the two must not
    be conflated. Every Commitments table alive today was written by hand, and
    treating those rows as hand-edits would make the first write on every real
    project a wall of reconcile prompts about work that was done correctly
    under the old rules. `perry-state § reconcile_drift` makes exactly this
    correction for board rows and gives the reason: a check people learn to
    ignore is worse than no check.
    """
    seen = commitment_events(events, cid)
    return seen[-1].get("to") if seen else None


def append_event(project_root: Path, event: dict) -> bool:
    """Append one event. The canonical file has already landed.

    Same failure direction `perry-task § commit` chose and documents:
    `OKR.md` never disagrees with itself, and the derived record may be
    missing. Losing an event costs history resolution; losing the write costs
    the truth.
    """
    ev = events_path(project_root)
    try:
        ev.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
        with open(ev, "a") as fh:
            fh.write(json.dumps(event, ensure_ascii=False) + "\n")
        return True
    except OSError as exc:
        print(f"perry-goals: warning — OKR.md was written, but the event could "
              f"not be appended to {ev}: {exc}. State is correct; this row will "
              f"read as unrecorded until the log is writable again.",
              file=sys.stderr)
        return False


def plain(x):
    return asdict(x) if is_dataclass(x) else x


def kr_rows(snap, events: list | None = None,
            events_present: bool = False) -> list[dict]:
    """Every KR, flat, with its level and objective attached.

    Three sources, deliberately merged in one pass rather than three shapes:
    `OKR.md`'s overall KRs, the current phase file's KRs, and — where the
    linkage register carries them — `target` / `current` / `tasks`. A KR that
    appears in both the phase file and the register is one KR; the register's
    numbers win, because they are the ones a task edge actually updated.

    **`current` arrives with its provenance from TASK-120.** The register's
    numbers are hand-written and nothing re-derives them, so each row also
    carries who asserted the number and when, whether a linked task has moved
    since, and — separately, never folded in — how many of the linked tasks are
    closed. `bin/lib § kr_progress_provenance` computes all three; see its
    comment for why the tally is beside `current` rather than inside it.
    """
    reg: dict[str, dict] = {}
    lk = getattr(snap, "linkage", None)
    for o in (getattr(lk, "objectives", None) or []):
        for k in (getattr(o, "krs", None) or []):
            reg[k.id] = {"target": k.target, "current": k.current,
                         "due": k.due, "tasks": list(k.tasks or [])}
    register_updated = getattr(lk, "updated", "") or ""
    status_by_id = lib.task_status_index(
        getattr(snap, "project_root", "."), getattr(snap, "board", None))

    out = []
    for level, src in (("overall", getattr(snap, "okr", None)),
                       ("phase", getattr(snap, "phase", None))):
        for o in (getattr(src, "objectives", None) or []):
            for k in (getattr(o, "krs", None) or []):
                r = reg.get(k.id, {})
                target, current = r.get("target"), r.get("current")
                out.append({
                    "id": k.id,
                    "level": level,
                    "objective": getattr(o, "title", ""),
                    "title": getattr(k, "text", "") or getattr(k, "title", ""),
                    "metric": getattr(k, "metric", ""),
                    "due": r.get("due", "") or getattr(k, "due", "") or "",
                    "qualifier": getattr(k, "qualifier", ""),
                    "linked_to": getattr(k, "linked", "") or "",
                    "stretch": bool(getattr(k, "stretch", False)),
                    # Numbers or null. NEVER a pre-computed percentage: half
                    # of a real OKR's targets are ceilings, not goals, and a
                    # max-drawdown limit rendered two-thirds achieved is the
                    # worst thing a dashboard can say. Only the project knows
                    # which direction a KR runs, so only the project may draw
                    # the bar. `progress` shipped in 1.0 and is removed here —
                    # that is why this is 2.0 and not 1.1.
                    "target": target,
                    "current": current,
                    "task_ids": r.get("tasks", []),
                    **lib.kr_progress_provenance(
                        current, r.get("tasks", []),
                        register_updated=register_updated,
                        status_by_id=status_by_id,
                        events=events, events_present=events_present),
                })
    return out


def days_since(value: str) -> int | None:
    m = re.search(r"\d{4}-\d{2}-\d{2}", value or "")
    if not m:
        return None
    try:
        return (date.today() - datetime.strptime(m.group(0), "%Y-%m-%d").date()).days
    except ValueError:
        return None


def objective_rows(src, lk) -> list[dict]:
    """Objectives with an id where one is *stated*, never invented.

    `OKR.md`'s objectives carry a title and no id — the heading is
    `### Objective 1 — <title>`, and the "1" is ordinal prose, not a handle.
    The linkage register does carry real ids, so an id is taken from there by
    title match and left empty otherwise. Deriving `O1`, `O2` from position
    would be minting a key the file never stated, and a consumer would key on
    it right up until someone reordered two headings.
    """
    by_title = {o.title.strip(): o.id
                for o in (getattr(lk, "objectives", None) or [])
                if getattr(o, "title", "")}
    out = []
    for o in (getattr(src, "objectives", None) or []):
        title = getattr(o, "title", "")
        out.append({"id": by_title.get(title.strip(), ""),
                    "title": title,
                    "krs": [k.id for k in (getattr(o, "krs", None) or [])]})
    return out


def build(project_root: Path, state_root: Path, level: str | None) -> dict:
    # `load_snapshot` takes the STATE root — it reads `root / "OKR.md"` directly.
    # Passing `project_root` made this tool read the wrong directory on every
    # project whose state root is not `.`, and report `okr_present: false` with
    # a payload that looked entirely well-formed. Perry and aiMark both keep
    # their state under `perry/`; both were reported as having no goals, and
    # that number was published to a consumer as measured data.
    snap = P.load_snapshot(state_root)
    okr, ph, lk = snap.okr, snap.phase, getattr(snap, "linkage", None)

    # `present`, `day` and `kr_total` are NOT fields on the parser dataclasses —
    # `perry-state` derives them in its own builder. Reading them with
    # `getattr(x, "present", False)` returned the default on every project and
    # said so to nobody: a live OKR reported `okr_present: false`. Derived here
    # explicitly, from fields that do exist.
    okr_present = bool(okr and (okr.version or okr.mission or okr.objectives))
    phase_day = days_since(ph.started) if ph else None
    phase_krs = sum(len(o.krs or []) for o in (ph.objectives or [])) if ph else None

    # The event log answers ONE question here: has a linked task moved since
    # the register asserted its numbers. `present` is carried separately from
    # the events themselves because "nothing moved" and "there is no log to ask"
    # are different facts, and a KR that reports the first when it means the
    # second is the shape this whole row exists about.
    events_file = events_path(project_root)
    krs = kr_rows(snap, read_events(project_root), events_file.exists())
    if level:
        krs = [k for k in krs if k["level"] == level]

    # Which source actually answered. A consumer showing a cascade needs to say
    # "no register" rather than "no progress" — they are different facts and
    # only one of them is about the work.
    if getattr(lk, "objectives", None):
        answered_by = "linkage"
    elif okr_present or ph is not None:
        answered_by = "prose"
    else:
        answered_by = "none"

    # DECLARED, never inferred by set arithmetic. A task absent from every KR's
    # `task_ids` might be unlinked, or might be a payload the consumer
    # truncated — only Perry can tell the two apart, so Perry says which.
    # `perry-state` computes this from the same snapshot; it is read here rather
    # than recomputed so the two cannot come to disagree.
    unlinked = []
    try:
        att = P.attribution(snap) if hasattr(P, "attribution") else None
    except Exception:
        att = None
    if att is None:
        linked_ids = {tid for k in krs for tid in k["task_ids"]}
        board = getattr(snap, "board", None)
        rows = (list(getattr(board, "p0", [])) + list(getattr(board, "p1", []))
                + list(getattr(board, "p2", []))) if board else []
        unlinked = sorted({t.id for t in rows if getattr(t, "id", "")
                           and t.id not in linked_ids})

    # Every one of these is a real state a project can be in, and none of them
    # is an error. A project may run goals with no phase, a phase with no
    # linkage register, or an OKR whose KRs carry no metric at all. Reported so
    # a consumer can say which, instead of rendering an empty panel that looks
    # like a failure.
    conformance = {
        "okr_present": okr_present,
        "phase_present": ph is not None,
        "linkage_present": bool(getattr(lk, "objectives", None)),
        "krs_without_metric": [k["id"] for k in krs if not k["metric"]],
        "krs_without_numbers": [k["id"] for k in krs
                                if k["target"] is None or k["current"] is None],
        "krs_not_in_linkage": [k["id"] for k in krs
                               if k["level"] == "phase" and not k["task_ids"]
                               and k["target"] is None],
        "duplicate_kr_ids": sorted(
            {k["id"] for k in krs if [x["id"] for x in krs].count(k["id"]) > 1}),
        # A KR whose linked tasks moved after its `current` was asserted. The
        # number is not wrong here — it is unverifiable, which is a different
        # thing and the only one Perry can establish. There is deliberately NO
        # companion entry for "current disagrees with the linked tasks": that
        # judgement needs the metric re-run, and inferring it from the edges is
        # the fabrication `perry/OKR.md § Operating Principles` forbids.
        "krs_with_stale_current": [k["id"] for k in krs
                                   if k["current_staleness"]["stale"]],
    }

    # Task→KR attribution is deliberately NOT here. It is a derived join over
    # the board, and `perry-state --json § attribution` already computes it from
    # the same snapshot. Recomputing it in a second place is how the two would
    # disagree; a consumer that needs it reads it there, and `krs[].tasks`
    # carries the edges this file can state from the register alone.
    return {
        "contract": LIST_CONTRACT,
        # Sorted here rather than trusted to writing order, the same guard
        # `bin/perry-task` puts on its own list: it is consumed as
        # "everything newer than the minor I tested against", which is a
        # slice only while the array is sorted.
        "semantics": sorted(
            LIST_SEMANTICS,
            key=lambda entry: tuple(int(x)
                                    for x in entry["version"].split("."))),
        "project_root": str(project_root),
        "state_root": str(state_root),
        "conformance": conformance,
        "okr": {
            "present": okr_present,
            "version": (okr.version if okr else "") or "",
            "mission": (okr.mission if okr else "") or "",
            "operating_principles": list(getattr(okr, "operating_principles", []) or []),
            "anti_goals": list(getattr(okr, "anti_goals", []) or []),
            "objectives": objective_rows(okr, lk),
        },
        "phase": None if ph is None else {
            "id": getattr(ph, "number", ""),
            "name": getattr(ph, "slug", ""),
            "number": getattr(ph, "number", ""),
            "slug": getattr(ph, "slug", ""),
            "status": getattr(ph, "status", ""),
            "started": getattr(ph, "started", ""),
            "day": phase_day,
            "kr_total": phase_krs,
            "cost_ceiling": getattr(ph, "cost_ceiling_raw", "") or "",
            "objectives": objective_rows(ph, lk),
        },
        "krs": krs,
        "answered_by": answered_by,
        "unlinked_task_ids": unlinked,
        "linkage": {
            "present": bool(getattr(lk, "objectives", None)),
            "phase": getattr(lk, "phase", "") or "",
            "updated": getattr(lk, "updated", "") or "",
            "error": getattr(lk, "error", "") or "",
        },
        "counts": {
            "objectives": len(getattr(okr, "objectives", None) or []),
            "krs": len(krs),
            "stretch": sum(1 for k in krs if k["stretch"]),
        },
    }


# ── commit: the commitments register ──────────────────────────────────────
#
# `goals/reference/phases.md § commit <promise>` is this section's
# specification, rule for rule, and TASK-042 closes when that page stops being
# an agent procedure. Every refusal below quotes the sentence it implements.

#: The two column sets, read from the schema rather than restated here.
# ── phase/<NNN>-linkage.md, edited in place ───────────────────────────────
#
# TASK-119. `goals/SKILL.md § State files` has called this file "machine-
# written" since the lane shipped, and until now nothing wrote it: this
# project's own `perry/phase/002-linkage.md` was typed by hand on 2026-08-20,
# every edge and every number. What follows is that missing writer, and it
# carries two rules from the surrounding documents rather than inventing its
# own.
#
# **Never guess an attribution.** `reference/okr-linkage.md`'s one rule:
# resolve by declared edge, then exact Project id, then registered alias, and
# if that does not yield exactly ONE KR, refuse and name the candidates. There
# is no fourth step. A writer that guesses is worse than no writer, because a
# guessed edge is indistinguishable from a declared one the moment it is
# written — and `bin/perry-state § resolve_kr` will then resolve every future
# progress report through it, with no inference of its own to blame.
#
# **`current` is an assertion, so it is never invented.** `target` / `current`
# are numbers or ABSENT. TASK-120 measured what a default costs: six of eight
# phase KRs in this project carry `target: 0`, so an unasserted `current`
# defaulting to `0` makes every drive-to-zero KR read as met on the day the
# register is written. Nothing here writes either field.


def _indent(line: str) -> int:
    return len(line) - len(line.lstrip(" "))


def _unquote(tok: str) -> str:
    """A scalar the way `parsers.parse_yaml_subset § scalar` reads one.

    Quotes are stripped when they match at both ends and nothing else happens
    — that reader does no escape processing and strips no inline comment, so
    neither does this. Anything it would have coerced (a number, `true`) is
    left as text here, because the values this editor moves are ids and names
    and the semantic comparison is made against the parsed model, not here.
    """
    tok = tok.strip()
    if len(tok) > 1 and tok[0] in "\"'" and tok[-1] == tok[0]:
        return tok[1:-1]
    return tok


def _segments(inner: str) -> list[tuple[int, int]]:
    """Top-level comma-separated spans of an inline flow body, with offsets.

    The same quote-aware scan `parsers._split_flow` makes, keeping the offsets
    it discards. An editor that disagreed with the reader about where an item
    ends would splice inside one.
    """
    out, start, quote = [], 0, ""
    for i, ch in enumerate(inner):
        if quote:
            if ch == quote:
                quote = ""
        elif ch in "\"'":
            quote = ch
        elif ch == ",":
            out.append((start, i))
            start = i + 1
    out.append((start, len(inner)))
    return out


def flow_items(inner: str) -> list[tuple[int, int]]:
    """(start, end) of each non-empty item in an inline flow body."""
    spans = []
    for a, b in _segments(inner):
        seg = inner[a:b]
        lead = len(seg) - len(seg.lstrip())
        trail = len(seg) - len(seg.rstrip())
        if b - trail > a + lead:
            spans.append((a + lead, b - trail))
    return spans


def flow_span(line: str, after: int) -> tuple[int, int] | None:
    """(open, close) offsets of the `[...]` starting after `after`, or None."""
    depth, start, quote = 0, None, ""
    for i in range(after, len(line)):
        ch = line[i]
        if quote:
            if ch == quote:
                quote = ""
            continue
        if ch in "\"'":
            quote = ch
        elif ch == "[":
            if depth == 0:
                start = i
            depth += 1
        elif ch == "]":
            depth -= 1
            if depth == 0 and start is not None:
                return start, i
    return None


#: A value that can be written into a flow list without quotes. Deliberately
#: narrow: anything the reader would coerce (`12`, `true`, `~`) or that its
#: splitter would cut in half (`,`, `]`) is quoted instead.
BARE_SAFE = re.compile(r"^[A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9_./+-]*$")


def render_scalar(value: str, like: str = "") -> str:
    """One scalar, quoted the way its neighbours are.

    `like` is an existing item from the same list — the register's own style,
    which is the only style this writer has an opinion about. Perry's files
    quote task ids (`["TASK-038", "TASK-088"]`); `reference/okr-linkage.md`'s
    example does not (`tasks: [REL-001]`). Both are read identically, and a
    writer that normalised one into the other would rewrite a line it was not
    asked to touch.
    """
    quote = '"'
    if like:
        like = like.strip()
        if like[:1] in "\"'" and like[-1:] == like[:1] and len(like) > 1:
            quote = like[0]
        elif BARE_SAFE.match(like) and BARE_SAFE.match(value):
            return value
    return f"{quote}{value}{quote}"


def check_writable(value: str, what: str) -> str:
    """Refuse a value this file cannot carry, rather than write a broken one.

    The reader strips matching outer quotes and does nothing else — it has no
    escape processing at all — so a value containing the quote character it
    would be written with cannot be read back as itself. That is a refusal and
    not a best effort: a half-written name in the attribution registry is the
    one place this project has said, three documents deep, that a wrong answer
    is worse than no answer.
    """
    value = (value or "").strip()
    if not value:
        raise Refused(f"{what} is empty. Nothing was written")
    if "\n" in value or "\r" in value:
        raise Refused(
            f"{what} contains a line break. A register value is one line of "
            f"YAML. Nothing was written")
    if '"' in value or "'" in value:
        raise Refused(
            f"{what} contains a quote character, and this register's reader "
            f"(`parsers.parse_yaml_subset`) does no escape processing — the "
            f"value could not be read back as itself. Nothing was written")
    return value


class Register:
    """`phase/<NNN>-linkage.md` as a list of lines, edited in place.

    The same rule `Okr` above states, for the same reason and with one
    difference that makes it stricter rather than looser. `OKR.md` is prose a
    human argued with; this file is YAML a machine reads on both sides — so
    the cost of re-rendering it is not lost wording, it is a **semantic** slip
    nobody can see: a re-serialiser that dropped a key, reordered a list or
    coerced `target: 0` back onto a KR whose author never asserted one would
    produce a file that still parses, still lints, and no longer says what it
    said. So: no round trip. Lines are located, one line (or one span inside
    one line) is spliced, and every other byte is the byte that was read.

    Two checks stand behind that, and both run before anything is written:

    - **the locator and the reader must agree.** Every entry the parsed model
      carries must be found on a line, and vice versa; a mismatch is refused
      rather than guessed at.
    - **the write must produce exactly the intended model.** `verify` re-reads
      the edited text with `parsers.parse_linkage` — the same reader Perry and
      `perry-lint` use — and compares it against the before-model with the one
      intended mutation applied. Anything else, including a change this writer
      made by accident, is refused with nothing written.
    """

    KINDS = {"objectives": "objective", "krs": "kr",
             "projects": "project", "agents": "agent"}

    def __init__(self, path: Path):
        self.path = path
        if not path.exists():
            raise Refused(
                f"no linkage register at {path}. `plan-phase` writes it from "
                f"`goals/state/linkage_TEMPLATE.md` when the phase opens "
                f"(goals/reference/linkage.md § The four moments it changes). "
                f"Nothing was written")
        # **Bytes, not `read_text()`.** Text mode is universal-newlines: it
        # would hand this editor a CRLF file with every `\r` already stripped,
        # the guard below would never fire, and the write would silently
        # convert the whole register to LF — a diff on every line of a file
        # this tool was asked to append one item to.
        self.text = path.read_bytes().decode("utf-8")
        if "\r" in self.text:
            raise Refused(
                f"{path.name} has CRLF line endings; this writer splices LF "
                f"lines and would leave the file mixed. Nothing was written")
        self.lines = self.text.split("\n")
        self.model = P.parse_linkage(self.text)
        if not self.model.ok:
            raise Refused(
                f"{path.name} cannot be read ({self.model.error or 'no spec'}) "
                f"— and a register Perry cannot read is one this tool must not "
                f"append to, because the append would be judged against a graph "
                f"nobody can see. Fix it, or run `perry-lint --root .` for the "
                f"line. Nothing was written")
        self.lo, self.hi = self._fence()
        self.entries = self._locate()
        self.edits: list[tuple[int, str, str]] = []

    # -- reading

    def render(self) -> str:
        """Exactly what `__init__` read, unless a line was edited.

        `split("\n")` / `join("\n")` round-trips a file with no trailing
        newline and one with several; `splitlines()` does not. Same reason as
        `Okr.render`, and the same test.
        """
        return "\n".join(self.lines)

    def unchanged(self) -> bool:
        return self.render() == self.text

    def _fence(self) -> tuple[int, int]:
        """(first, last) line indexes of the frontmatter BODY, exclusive end."""
        if not re.match(r"\A\ufeff?---[ \t]*$", self.lines[0] if self.lines else ""):
            raise Refused(
                f"{self.path.name} does not open with a `---` fence, so it "
                f"carries no frontmatter to write. Nothing was written")
        for i in range(1, len(self.lines)):
            if re.match(r"^---[ \t]*$", self.lines[i]):
                return 1, i
        raise Refused(
            f"{self.path.name}'s frontmatter fence is never closed. "
            f"Nothing was written")

    def _live(self, i: int) -> bool:
        """A line the reader looks at — not blank, not a whole-line comment."""
        s = self.lines[i].strip()
        return bool(s) and not s.startswith("#")

    def _container(self, i: int, indent: int) -> str:
        """The key whose list this `- ` item at `indent` belongs to."""
        for j in range(i - 1, self.lo - 1, -1):
            if not self._live(j):
                continue
            if _indent(self.lines[j]) < indent:
                m = re.match(r"^\s*([A-Za-z_][\w.-]*)\s*:\s*$", self.lines[j])
                return m.group(1) if m else ""
        return ""

    def _item_end(self, start: int, indent: int) -> int:
        """First line after `start` that is outside this list item."""
        for j in range(start + 1, self.hi):
            if not self._live(j):
                continue
            if _indent(self.lines[j]) <= indent:
                return j
        return self.hi

    def _locate(self) -> dict[str, list[dict]]:
        """Every entry the model carries, found on a line.

        The model comes from the reader; the LINE comes from here, and the two
        are reconciled before any edit. This is the one place a second opinion
        about the file's shape could enter, so it is also the one place that
        refuses instead of proceeding when the opinions differ.
        """
        found: dict[str, list[dict]] = {k: [] for k in self.KINDS.values()}
        for i in range(self.lo, self.hi):
            m = re.match(r"^(\s*)-\s+id\s*:\s*(.+?)\s*$", self.lines[i])
            if not m:
                continue
            indent = len(m.group(1))
            kind = self.KINDS.get(self._container(i, indent))
            if not kind:
                continue
            found[kind].append({
                "id": _unquote(m.group(2)), "start": i, "indent": indent,
                "end": self._item_end(i, indent)})
        expected = {
            "objective": [o.id for o in self.model.objectives],
            "kr": [k.id for o in self.model.objectives for k in o.krs],
            "project": [p.project_id for p in self.model.projects],
            "agent": [a.id for a in self.model.agents]}
        for kind, ids in expected.items():
            got = [e["id"] for e in found[kind]]
            if got != ids:
                raise Refused(
                    f"this writer could not find every {kind} entry of "
                    f"{self.path.name} on a line — the reader sees {ids} and "
                    f"the line scan sees {got}. That disagreement is this "
                    f"tool's bug or a shape it was never taught, and either "
                    f"way it must not edit by line number here. "
                    f"Nothing was written")
        return found

    def entry(self, kind: str, ident: str) -> dict | None:
        for e in self.entries[kind]:
            if e["id"] == ident:
                return e
        return None

    def child_indent(self, entry: dict) -> int:
        for j in range(entry["start"] + 1, entry["end"]):
            if self._live(j):
                return _indent(self.lines[j])
        return entry["indent"] + 2

    def last_content_line(self, entry: dict) -> int:
        """The last line the reader looks at inside this entry.

        Where a key the entry does not carry yet is inserted. Not `end - 1`:
        an entry may be followed by a blank line or a comment, and putting
        `tasks:` after one of those would move it out of the entry.
        """
        last = entry["start"]
        for j in range(entry["start"] + 1, entry["end"]):
            if self._live(j):
                last = j
        return last

    def key_line(self, name: str, lo: int, hi: int, indent: int) -> int | None:
        """The line carrying `<name>:` at exactly `indent`, in [lo, hi)."""
        for j in range(lo, hi):
            if not self._live(j) or _indent(self.lines[j]) != indent:
                continue
            if re.match(rf"^\s*{re.escape(name)}\s*:(\s|$)", self.lines[j]):
                return j
        return None

    def top_key_line(self, name: str) -> int | None:
        return self.key_line(name, self.lo, self.hi, 0)

    # -- editing
    #
    # Every mutation below goes through `_set` or `_insert`, so the record of
    # what this write touched is kept by construction rather than recovered by
    # diffing afterwards. `--dry-run` prints it.

    def set_line(self, i: int, line: str) -> None:
        self.edits.append((i, self.lines[i], line))
        self.lines[i] = line

    def insert_line(self, i: int, line: str) -> None:
        """Insert, and move every index this object holds that sits after it.

        The fence and the located entries are line numbers, and a writer that
        inserted a line without moving them would make its *second* edit at an
        offset that was correct before its first. `verify` would catch it; the
        bookkeeping is here so it never has to."""
        self.edits.append((i, None, line))
        self.lines[i:i] = [line]
        if i <= self.hi:
            self.hi += 1
        for entries in self.entries.values():
            for e in entries:
                e["start"] += (e["start"] >= i)
                e["end"] += (e["end"] >= i)

    def delete_line(self, i: int) -> None:
        self.edits.append((i, self.lines[i], None))
        del self.lines[i]
        if i < self.hi:
            self.hi -= 1
        for entries in self.entries.values():
            for e in entries:
                e["start"] -= (e["start"] > i)
                e["end"] -= (e["end"] > i)

    def block_items(self, i: int, indent: int) -> list[int]:
        """Line indexes of a block list written beneath the key on line `i`."""
        out = []
        for j in range(i + 1, self.hi):
            if not self._live(j):
                continue
            ind = _indent(self.lines[j])
            if ind <= indent:
                break
            if self.lines[j].lstrip().startswith("- "):
                out.append(j)
        return out

    def append_to_list(self, i: int, indent: int, value: str) -> None:
        """Append one scalar to the list on line `i` — flow or block.

        Three shapes, because all three are legal and Perry's own files carry
        two of them: `tasks: []`, `tasks: ["A"]`, and a block list of `- `
        lines. In every case the bytes that survive are every byte that is not
        the one item being added.
        """
        line = self.lines[i]
        colon = line.index(":", _indent(line))
        span = flow_span(line, colon)
        if span:
            open_at, close_at = span
            inner = line[open_at + 1:close_at]
            items = flow_items(inner)
            if not items:
                new_inner = render_scalar(value)
            else:
                last = inner[items[-1][0]:items[-1][1]]
                sep = ", " if re.search(r",\s", inner) else ("," if "," in inner else ", ")
                tail = len(inner) - len(inner.rstrip())
                new_inner = (inner[:len(inner) - tail] + sep
                             + render_scalar(value, last) + inner[len(inner) - tail:])
            self.set_line(i, line[:open_at + 1] + new_inner + line[close_at:])
            return
        rows = self.block_items(i, indent)
        if rows:
            at = rows[-1]
            like = self.lines[at].lstrip()[2:]
            self.insert_line(at + 1, " " * _indent(self.lines[at]) + "- "
                         + render_scalar(value, like))
            return
        if line[colon + 1:].strip():
            raise Refused(
                f"`{line.strip()}` is not a list this writer can append to — "
                f"it carries a scalar. Nothing was written")
        self.set_line(i, line.rstrip() + " [" + render_scalar(value) + "]")

    def remove_from_list(self, i: int, indent: int, value: str) -> None:
        """Remove one scalar from the list on line `i`. The inverse, same rules."""
        line = self.lines[i]
        colon = line.index(":", _indent(line))
        span = flow_span(line, colon)
        if span:
            open_at, close_at = span
            inner = line[open_at + 1:close_at]
            items = flow_items(inner)
            for n, (a, b) in enumerate(items):
                if _unquote(inner[a:b]) != value:
                    continue
                if n + 1 < len(items):        # take the separator after it
                    cut_a, cut_b = a, items[n + 1][0]
                elif n:                        # or the one before it
                    cut_a, cut_b = items[n - 1][1], b
                else:                          # the only item
                    cut_a, cut_b = a, b
                self.set_line(i, line[:open_at + 1] + inner[:cut_a] + inner[cut_b:]
                          + line[close_at:])
                return
            return
        for j in self.block_items(i, indent):
            if _unquote(self.lines[j].lstrip()[2:]) == value:
                self.delete_line(j)
                return

    def insert_top_key(self, line: str) -> int:
        """A top-level key the register does not carry yet, before the fence.

        Placed last rather than in schema order on purpose: every existing key
        keeps its line, so a register whose author grouped or commented the
        frontmatter reads afterwards the way it read before, with one line
        added at the end of the block.
        """
        at = self.hi
        while at > self.lo and not self._live(at - 1):
            at -= 1
        self.insert_line(at, line)
        return at

    def set_updated(self, stamp: str) -> None:
        """`updated` to a full ISO datetime, in whatever style it is written in.

        Every write bumps it (`goals/reference/linkage.md § The four moments`),
        and a day-only value is dropped by both readers rather than guessed at
        — so this always writes the time as well.
        """
        i = self.top_key_line("updated")
        if i is None:
            self.insert_top_key(f'updated: "{stamp}"')
            return
        head, _, rest = self.lines[i].partition(":")
        lead = len(rest) - len(rest.lstrip(" "))
        self.set_line(i, f"{head}:{rest[:lead]}"
                  + render_scalar(stamp, rest.strip() or '""'))

    # -- the gate

    def verify(self, expect: dict) -> None:
        """Re-read what this write produced, and refuse anything unintended.

        The counterpart of `write_okr_and_store`'s byte comparison, one layer
        up: there, the store must reproduce the file; here, the file must
        reproduce the model. Both run **before** anything lands, and both
        exist because the failure they catch is invisible in the artefact — a
        register that parses and says something slightly different is not
        something a reviewer can see by reading the diff.
        """
        after = P.parse_linkage(self.render())
        if not after.ok:
            raise Refused(
                f"the write would produce a register Perry could not read "
                f"({after.error}). That is this writer's bug, not the file's. "
                f"Nothing was written")
        got = asdict(after)
        if got != expect:
            where = next((k for k in expect if got.get(k) != expect.get(k)), "?")
            raise Refused(
                f"the write would change more of the register than it was "
                f"asked to — the re-read disagrees with the intended graph at "
                f"`{where}`. That is this writer's bug, not the file's. "
                f"Nothing was written")

    def diff(self) -> list[str]:
        """The edits, as `-`/`+` lines. What `--dry-run` prints."""
        out = []
        for i, before, after in self.edits:
            if before is not None:
                out.append(f"-{i + 1:>4}  {before}")
            if after is not None:
                out.append(f"+{i + 1:>4}  {after}")
        return out


# ── link — accepting PMO's attribution hand-off ───────────────────────────


def register_path(state_root: Path) -> Path:
    """`phase/<NNN>-linkage.md` for the CURRENT phase.

    The same three lines `parsers.load_snapshot` uses to find the register it
    parses — deliberately the same rule, because a writer that picked a
    different file from the one Perry reads would append edges nothing
    resolves. It is restated rather than imported because `load_snapshot`
    computes it inside a 90-line function and returns the parsed graph, not
    the path; a third caller is the moment to extract it into `parsers`, not
    the moment to copy it again.
    """
    pointer = state_root / "phase" / "CURRENT"
    slug = pointer.read_text().strip() if pointer.exists() else ""
    if not slug or slug in {"(none)", "none", "—"}:
        raise Refused(
            "there is no current phase (`phase/CURRENT` is empty or missing), "
            "so there is no linkage register to write. `/perry goals "
            "plan-phase <slug>` opens one. Nothing was written")
    number = slug.split("-")[0]
    return state_root / "phase" / f"{number}-linkage.md"


def kr_ids(model) -> list[str]:
    return [k.id for o in model.objectives for k in o.krs]


def objective_of(model, kr_id: str) -> str:
    for o in model.objectives:
        for k in o.krs:
            if k.id == kr_id:
                return o.id
    return ""


def resolve_target(model, token: str, where: str) -> str:
    """One KR id, or a refusal naming the candidates. Never a near match.

    `reference/okr-linkage.md § Resolution order`, which `perry-state §
    resolve_kr` already applies on the read side: a declared KR id, then an
    exact Project id, then an exact registered alias — stopping at the first
    step that yields exactly ONE KR. There is no fourth step, and the reason
    is worth keeping next to the code: the whole registry exists so that a
    name which drifted resolves through a recorded alias instead of through a
    resemblance. A writer that fell back to "closest match" would put a
    guessed edge into the one file both Perry and the frontend treat as
    declared truth, where nothing downstream can tell it from a real one.
    """
    ids = kr_ids(model)
    if token in ids:
        return token

    def candidates(match) -> list[tuple[str, str]]:
        out = []
        for p in model.projects:
            if not match(p) or not p.serves_kr:
                continue
            out.append((p.serves_kr, f'{p.serves_kr} (project {p.project_id} '
                                     f'"{p.name}", {p.status})'))
        return out

    live = {id(p) for p in model.projects
            if p.status not in {"dropped", "unlinked"}}
    for label, match in (
            ("Project id", lambda p: p.project_id == token),
            ("name or alias", lambda p: token.strip().lower() in
                [n.lower() for n in [p.name, *p.aliases] if n])):
        hits = candidates(lambda p: match(p) and id(p) in live)
        distinct = sorted({kr for kr, _ in hits})
        if len(distinct) == 1:
            return distinct[0]
        if len(distinct) > 1:
            raise Refused(
                f"{token!r} does not resolve to exactly one KR — it matches "
                f"{len(distinct)} by {label}: "
                + "; ".join(text for _, text in hits) +
                f". Attribution resolves by declared edge, then exact Project "
                f"id, then registered alias, and never by resemblance "
                f"(reference/okr-linkage.md § The one rule). Ask the user "
                f"which KR {where} serves and re-run with that KR id. "
                f"Nothing was written")
        retired = candidates(lambda p: match(p) and id(p) not in live)
        if retired:
            raise Refused(
                f"{token!r} matches only a retired Project by {label}: "
                + "; ".join(text for _, text in retired) +
                f". A dropped Project's KR edge is history, not an "
                f"attribution for new work. Ask the user which KR {where} "
                f"serves. Nothing was written")
    raise Refused(
        f"{token!r} is not a KR id, a Project id or a registered alias in "
        f"this phase's graph. Its KR ids are: {', '.join(ids) or '(none)'}. A "
        f"near-match is not a match — attribution is never guessed from a "
        f"name (reference/okr-linkage.md § The one rule). Ask the user which "
        f"KR {where} serves, or declare it unlinked with "
        f"`link --unlinked {where}`. Nothing was written")


def claimed_by(model, label: str) -> str:
    """Which Project already answers to this name or alias, if any.

    Case-insensitively, because `perry-lint § linkage-names-unique` folds case
    before comparing and a writer that did not would create the exact file the
    linter rejects.
    """
    key = label.strip().lower()
    for p in model.projects:
        if key in [n.strip().lower() for n in [p.name, *p.aliases] if n]:
            return p.project_id
    return ""


def link_edge(reg: Register, expect: dict, task: str, token: str) -> dict:
    """1 — resolved → append the edge (`goals/reference/linkage.md § link`)."""
    kr = resolve_target(reg.model, token, task)
    if kr not in kr_ids(reg.model):
        raise Refused(
            f"{token!r} resolves to {kr}, which this phase's graph does not "
            f"carry as a KR — the Project registry and the objectives "
            f"disagree. Fix the graph first (`perry-lint --root .`). "
            f"Nothing was written")
    holder = reg.model.kr_for_task(task)
    if holder == kr:
        return {"already": f"{task} is already linked to {kr}"}
    if holder:
        raise Refused(
            f"{task} is already listed under {holder}; linking it to {kr} as "
            f"well would make its attribution ambiguous, and `perry-lint` "
            f"rejects that file (`linkage-task-single-kr`). Move it, don't "
            f"duplicate it — remove the edge under {holder} first, on the "
            f"user's confirmation. Nothing was written")
    entry = reg.entry("kr", kr)
    indent = reg.child_indent(entry)
    line = reg.key_line("tasks", entry["start"], entry["end"], indent)
    if line is None:
        line = reg.last_content_line(entry) + 1
        reg.insert_line(line, " " * indent + "tasks: []")
    reg.append_to_list(line, indent, task)
    for o in expect["objectives"]:
        for k in o["krs"]:
            if k["id"] == kr:
                k["tasks"] = list(k["tasks"]) + [task]
    out = {"kr": kr, "task": task}
    # "If the task is currently in `unlinked[]`, remove it there in the same
    # edit — otherwise it renders as both attributed and drifting."
    if task in reg.model.unlinked:
        at = reg.top_key_line("unlinked")
        if at is not None:
            reg.remove_from_list(at, 0, task)
        expect["unlinked"] = [t for t in expect["unlinked"] if t != task]
        out["undeclared_unlinked"] = True
    return out


def link_unlinked(reg: Register, expect: dict, task: str) -> dict:
    """3 — unresolved → declare it unlinked. A DECLARATION, never inferred."""
    holder = reg.model.kr_for_task(task)
    if holder:
        raise Refused(
            f"{task} is linked to {holder}. Declaring it unlinked as well "
            f"would render it as both attributed and drifting, and the two "
            f"lists would disagree about the same task. Remove the edge under "
            f"{holder} first, on the user's confirmation. Nothing was written")
    if task in reg.model.unlinked:
        return {"already": f"{task} is already declared unlinked"}
    at = reg.top_key_line("unlinked")
    if at is None:
        at = reg.insert_top_key("unlinked: []")
    reg.append_to_list(at, 0, task)
    expect["unlinked"] = list(expect["unlinked"]) + [task]
    return {"task": task, "unlinked": True}


def link_alias(reg: Register, expect: dict, pid: str, name: str) -> dict:
    """2 — a name was confirmed as an existing Project → append the alias.

    Only on the user's confirmation, which this tool cannot check and does not
    pretend to: what it can check is that the name is not already somebody
    else's, which is the ambiguity the registry exists to prevent.
    """
    project = next((p for p in reg.model.projects if p.project_id == pid), None)
    if project is None:
        raise Refused(
            f"no Project {pid} in this phase's graph. Its Project ids are: "
            f"{', '.join(p.project_id for p in reg.model.projects) or '(none)'}"
            f". A new one is registered with `link --project <PROJECT-ID> "
            f"<KR-ID> \"<name>\"`. Nothing was written")
    owner = claimed_by(reg.model, name)
    if owner == pid:
        return {"already": f"{pid} already answers to {name!r}"}
    if owner:
        raise Refused(
            f"{name!r} is already claimed by {owner}. Two Projects answering "
            f"to one name is the duplicate-name trap the registry exists to "
            f"prevent — attribution could not resolve it to one KR, and "
            f"`perry-lint` rejects it (`linkage-names-unique`). Nothing was "
            f"written")
    entry = reg.entry("project", pid)
    indent = reg.child_indent(entry)
    line = reg.key_line("aliases", entry["start"], entry["end"], indent)
    if line is None:
        line = reg.last_content_line(entry) + 1
        reg.insert_line(line, " " * indent + "aliases: []")
    reg.append_to_list(line, indent, name)
    for p in expect["projects"]:
        if p["project_id"] == pid:
            p["aliases"] = list(p["aliases"]) + [name]
    return {"project": pid, "alias": name}


def link_project(reg: Register, expect: dict, pid: str, kr: str,
                 name: str) -> dict:
    """4 — a new Project appeared mid-phase → append it to the registry."""
    if any(p.project_id == pid for p in reg.model.projects):
        raise Refused(
            f"Project {pid} is already in this phase's graph. A Project "
            f"serves exactly one KR; if this is a second one, it needs its "
            f"own id. Nothing was written")
    if kr not in kr_ids(reg.model):
        raise Refused(
            f"{kr} is not a KR id in this phase's graph. Its KR ids are: "
            f"{', '.join(kr_ids(reg.model)) or '(none)'}. A Project is "
            f"registered against a declared KR, never against a new one. "
            f"Nothing was written")
    owner = claimed_by(reg.model, name)
    if owner:
        raise Refused(
            f"{name!r} is already claimed by {owner}. Two Projects answering "
            f"to one name is the duplicate-name trap the registry exists to "
            f"prevent (`perry-lint § linkage-names-unique`). If this is the "
            f"same Project under a new name, that is an alias: `link --alias "
            f"{owner} \"{name}\"`. Nothing was written")
    # `objective` is DERIVED from the KR id — `P002-O1-KR2` → `O1` — and then
    # checked against the objective the KR actually sits under. The linter
    # checks the first agreement (`linkage-objective-agrees`); the second is
    # this writer refusing to add a row to a graph whose own nesting already
    # disagrees with its ids, rather than picking a side.
    # Anchored end to end (TASK-180). The pre-2026-08-28 `P-O1.2` [[old-form]]
    # was prefix-matched here because its phase was nowhere in the id; the
    # new form has every segment labelled, so a partial match would only
    # ever be a malformed id being read halfway. Pinned by
    # `tests/test_linkage_writer.py § TestTheRefusalFires.
    # test_a_kr_id_that_disagrees_with_its_nesting_is_refused` — before
    # that test the anchor could be reverted with nothing going red,
    # because `objective` falls back to the nesting and the refusal below
    # is the only thing that notices.
    m = re.match(r"^P\d{3}-(O\d+)-KR\d+$", kr)
    nests_under = objective_of(reg.model, kr)
    objective = m.group(1) if m else nests_under
    if m and nests_under and objective != nests_under:
        raise Refused(
            f"{kr} encodes objective {objective} but sits under "
            f"{nests_under} in this graph. Whichever is wrong, this writer "
            f"cannot tell, and a Project entry would have to agree with one "
            f"of them. Fix the graph first (`perry-lint --root .`). "
            f"Nothing was written")
    at = reg.top_key_line("projects")
    if at is None:
        at = reg.insert_top_key("projects:")
    else:
        span = flow_span(reg.lines[at], reg.lines[at].index(":"))
        if span:
            if flow_items(reg.lines[at][span[0] + 1:span[1]]):
                raise Refused(
                    "`projects:` is written as an inline list of maps, which "
                    "this register's reader does not support (nested flow "
                    "maps are outside `parse_yaml_subset`). Nothing was "
                    "written")
            reg.set_line(at, reg.lines[at][:span[0]].rstrip())
    rows = reg.block_items(at, 0)
    indent = _indent(reg.lines[rows[-1]]) if rows else 2
    tail = reg.entries["project"][-1]["end"] if reg.entries["project"] \
        else at + 1
    block = [f'{" " * indent}- id: {render_scalar(pid)}',
             f'{" " * (indent + 2)}serves: {kr}']
    if objective:
        block.append(f'{" " * (indent + 2)}objective: {objective}')
    block += [f'{" " * (indent + 2)}name: {render_scalar(name)}',
              f'{" " * (indent + 2)}aliases: []',
              f'{" " * (indent + 2)}status: active']
    for n, line in enumerate(block):
        reg.insert_line(tail + n, line)
    expect["projects"] = list(expect["projects"]) + [{
        "project_id": pid, "serves_kr": kr, "objective": objective,
        "name": name, "aliases": [], "status": "active"}]
    return {"project": pid, "serves": kr, "objective": objective,
            "name": name}


LINK_MODES = ("alias", "unlinked", "project")


def cmd_link(args, ctx) -> dict:
    """`link` — the four moments `goals/reference/linkage.md` documents.

    One command, four shapes, and the argument grammar is that page's own:

        link <TASK-ID> <KR-ID>                     the edge
        link --alias <PROJECT-ID> "<name>"         a confirmed second name
        link --unlinked <TASK-ID>                  a declaration of drift
        link --project <PROJECT-ID> <KR-ID> "<name>"   a new Project
    """
    reg: Register = ctx["register"]
    rest = list(args.rest)
    modes = [m for m in LINK_MODES if getattr(args, m)]
    if len(modes) > 1:
        raise Refused(
            f"--{' and --'.join(modes)} are four different writes; run them "
            f"one at a time. Nothing was written")
    mode = modes[0] if modes else "edge"
    need = {"edge": 2, "alias": 2, "unlinked": 1, "project": 3}[mode]
    usage = {
        "edge": "link <TASK-ID> <KR-ID>",
        "alias": 'link --alias <PROJECT-ID> "<the other name>"',
        "unlinked": "link --unlinked <TASK-ID>",
        "project": 'link --project <PROJECT-ID> <KR-ID> "<name>"'}[mode]
    if len(rest) != need:
        why = ("no KR was named, and attribution is never inferred from a "
               "task id alone (reference/okr-linkage.md § The one rule) — "
               "`link --unlinked <TASK-ID>` is how an unresolved task is "
               "recorded. " if mode == "edge" and len(rest) < 2 else "")
        raise Refused(
            f"{why}{usage} takes {need} argument(s); {len(rest)} given. "
            f"Nothing was written")
    # **`link` takes its values positionally, so there is no flag to name** —
    # `goals/reference/linkage.md` writes the grammar that way and
    # `tests/test_procedures_call_the_tool.py` exists to keep the page and the
    # command in step. The slot is named instead, and it is read out of
    # `usage` rather than re-listed here, so the refusal cannot name a
    # placeholder the usage line above it does not show. `argument N` alone
    # was what the user got, and on a three-argument `--project` that is a
    # position to count out against a grammar they have to go and find.
    slots = re.findall(r"<[^>]+>", usage)
    values = [check_writable(v, f"{slots[n] if n < len(slots) else ''} "
                                f"(argument {n + 1})".strip())
              for n, v in enumerate(rest)]

    expect = asdict(reg.model)
    stamp = lib.register_stamp()
    if mode == "edge":
        result = link_edge(reg, expect, values[0], values[1])
    elif mode == "unlinked":
        result = link_unlinked(reg, expect, values[0])
    elif mode == "alias":
        result = link_alias(reg, expect, values[0], values[1])
    else:
        result = link_project(reg, expect, values[0], values[1], values[2])

    result["mode"] = mode
    result["file"] = str(reg.path)
    if result.get("already"):
        # Nothing to write, and saying so is the whole answer. A no-op that
        # bumped `updated` would report the graph as freshly asserted on a run
        # that changed nothing — the same lie `current: 0` tells one field over.
        result["written"] = False
        result["diff"] = []
        return result
    # **Bumping `updated` also re-dates every `current` in the register**, and
    # that is reported rather than absorbed. `goals/reference/linkage.md` says
    # every write bumps it, and both readers need it — but TASK-120's
    # `asserted_at` is read FROM it, at `asserted_scope: "register"`, because
    # there is no per-KR assertion date. So an edge appended to one KR moves
    # the staleness reference of numbers asserted weeks ago under every other
    # KR: a `current` whose linked task moved yesterday reported STALE before
    # this write and reads fresh after it, with nothing about the number
    # changed. One field is carrying two facts — when the graph last changed,
    # and when a number was arrived at — which is the same shape as the `By
    # when` column TASK-091 had to split into `due` + `by_when_note`.
    #
    # It is NOT fixed here: a per-KR assertion date is a new field in
    # `schema/state-schema.json`, which is behind this project's safety gate
    # (TASK-119 § verification 5). Naming it on every write is what keeps it
    # from being discovered as a silent number months later.
    redated = [k.id for o in reg.model.objectives for k in o.krs
               if k.current is not None]
    if redated:
        result["current_assertions_redated"] = redated
        if not args.as_json:
            print(f"perry-goals: ⚠ this write bumps `updated`, which is also "
                  f"where `current`'s assertion date is read from "
                  f"(`asserted_scope: register` — there is no per-KR date). "
                  f"{len(redated)} KR(s) with an asserted `current` will read "
                  f"as freshly asserted and lose any staleness signal: "
                  f"{', '.join(redated)}. Nothing about those numbers "
                  f"changed.", file=sys.stderr)
    reg.set_updated(stamp)
    expect["updated"] = stamp
    reg.verify(expect)
    result["updated"] = stamp
    result["diff"] = reg.diff()
    event = {"ts": lib.event_stamp(),
             "event": f"link-{mode}", "actor": args.actor,
             "file": reg.path.name,
             **{k: v for k, v in result.items()
                if k in ("task", "kr", "project", "alias", "serves", "name")}}
    result["event"] = event
    if args.dry_run:
        result["dry_run"] = True
        result["written"] = False
        return result
    write_atomic(ctx["state_root"], reg.path, reg.render())
    result["written"] = True
    result["event_written"] = append_event(ctx["project_root"], event)
    return result


def commitment_columns() -> tuple[list[str], list[str]]:
    spec = okr_table_spec(COMMITMENTS_UNDER)
    return list(spec.get("columns") or []), list(spec.get("optional_columns") or {})


#: Which flag a `## Commitments` cell's text arrived on. The register's own
#: column names are what both write paths already key on — `changes` on the
#: amend side, `values` on the create side — so this is the whole translation
#: from "the cell that could not be written" to "the words you typed".
#:
#: `Id` and `Status` are absent on purpose: neither is ever typed. `Id` is
#: minted here and `Status` is one of two literals, so a refusal naming a flag
#: for either would name a flag the caller does not have.
FLAG_FOR_COLUMN = {"Track": "--track", "Promise": "--promise",
                   "To whom": "--to", "Due": "--due",
                   "By when note": "--by-when-note",
                   "Discharged by": "--discharged-by"}


#: The typed half of the promise's clock, and the whole of what `Due` accepts.
#:
#: **The clock regex lived here and is gone** (ADR-007 decision 3, TASK-091).
#: It was one regex asked a natural-language question — *does this cell name a
#: clock?* — over a column holding both an ISO date and free prose. It failed five
#: V4 review rounds in four shapes; the last because `\b` does not exist in
#: Chinese, so the English half matched on word boundaries and the Chinese half
#: on bare substrings, and `下周期` ("next cycle") wrote a live commitment row
#: while `next cycle` was refused. No sixth round: the column split instead.
#:
#: What is left is a **format**, not a judgement. Two spellings, both closed:
#: an ISO date, or the `<n><unit>` SLA shorthand `.perry/config.md § Tracks`
#: already uses. Anything else is not "vague" — it is simply not this field,
#: and it belongs in `By when note`, which nothing validates. The refusal says
#: so and names the flag, so a user is never guessing where their words go.
SLA_TOKEN_RE = lib.SLA_TOKEN_RE  # see `lib.ISO_DATE_RE`

#: `lib.ISO_DATE_RE` — imported, not re-spelled. This module declared itself
#: "the one spelling" while `bin/perry-diagnose` carried a second, unanchored
#: one; a claim of uniqueness that a `grep` disproves is worse than no claim,
#: because it stops the next reader from grepping.
ISO_DATE_RE = lib.ISO_DATE_RE


def real_date(value: str) -> date | None:
    """The `Due` cell as a `date`, or `None` when it does not hold one.

    `None` covers both "empty" and "an SLA token" — neither is a calendar day,
    and every caller here wants a day it can compare against today.
    """
    text = lib.normalize_typed_cell(value)
    if not lib.is_iso_date(text):
        return None
    return date.fromisoformat(text)


def is_sla_token(value: str) -> bool:
    """`3d`, `2w`, `24h` — the shorthand `.perry/config.md § Tracks` writes."""
    return lib.is_sla_token(value)


def tracks_of(project_root: Path) -> list[dict]:
    """The declared tracks — from the STORE, `.perry/config.jsonl`.

    `bin/perry-state § declared_tracks` is the one reader (P003-O2-KR1): this
    used to parse `.perry/config.md § Tracks` directly, which is a projection
    of that store wherever it exists, so a register the store and the file
    disagreed about had two answers depending on which tool asked.

    The empty list stays the answer for a project with no config at all — it is
    what `track_named` turns into "this project declares no tracks", and
    `declared_tracks`' implicit `main` would instead accept every `--track main`
    on a project that never configured Perry.
    """
    perry = project_root / ".perry"
    if not (perry / "config.jsonl").exists() and not (perry / "config.md").exists():
        return []
    return perry_state().declared_tracks(project_root)


def track_named(tracks: list[dict], name: str, flag: str = "") -> dict:
    """The declared track `name`, or a refusal.

    `flag` is passed **only** where the name was typed by the caller. Three of
    the four call sites read it out of a `Track` cell the file already held —
    `--migrate` walking rows, and the amend path checking `--due` against the
    row's own track — and naming `--track` there would send the user to fix a
    flag they never passed.
    """
    for t in tracks:
        if t.get("track") == name:
            return t
    declared = [t.get("track") for t in tracks if t.get("declared")]
    subject = f"{flag} was given {name!r}, which is" if flag else \
        f"track {name!r} is"
    raise Refused(
        f"{subject} not declared in `.perry/config.md § Tracks`; "
        f"declared: {declared or '(none)'}. A commitment is filed against a "
        f"track, and an undeclared one has no mode and no SLA. Nothing was written")


def check_due(track: dict, value: str) -> None:
    """`phases.md § commit` step 4 — **one column, one value space.**

    | pipeline | an ISO date            | an SLA token is refused: triage
    |          |                        | compares this cell against today
    | queue    | an ISO date, or the    | anything else is refused, and the
    |          | track's SLA shorthand  | refusal names `--by-when-note`

    The predecessor asked whether the cell *named a clock*, over a column that
    also held prose. That is not a question a regex can answer, and the regex
    that tried proved it five times. Here the question is *is this cell an ISO
    date or an SLA token*, which has one answer in every language — and the
    prose that column also carried now has a field nothing asks anything.
    """
    # Both value refusals below name `--due` outright. They can, because
    # `value` is `args.due` at every call site — `--migrate` classifies the
    # file's own cells through `lib.classify_due` and never comes here.
    classification = lib.classify_due(track, value)
    if classification == lib.DUE_UNFILLED:
        raise Refused(
            "--due is required and has no default. A promise with no date is "
            "not a commitment — and `--by-when-note` is a note ABOUT the "
            "deadline, never a substitute for one. Nothing was written")
    if lib.due_track_missing_clock(track):
        raise Refused(
            f"track {track.get('track')!r} is `queue` mode and "
            f"`.perry/config.md § Tracks` gives it no `SLA`. "
            f"A commitment measured against the track's SLA, pointing at "
            f"an empty register, is a promise with no clock at all. Set "
            f"the track's SLA first. Nothing was written")
    if ((track.get("mode") or "project").lower() == "pipeline"
            and classification != lib.DUE_DATE):
        raise Refused(
            f"--due was given {value!r}, and track {track.get('track')!r} is "
            f"`pipeline` mode, so `Due` must be an ISO date (2026-09-30) — "
            f"triage compares this cell against today, and an SLA token has "
            f"no day in it. Nothing was written")
    if classification == lib.DUE_INVALID:
        raise Refused(
            f"--due was given {value!r}, and `Due` is typed: an ISO date "
            f"(2026-09-30) or an SLA token (3d, 2w, 24h). If that is how the "
            f"deadline was WORDED to the party — \"within the track SLA\", "
            f"\"same business day\" — it belongs in `--by-when-note`, which "
            f"is prose and is never validated. Nothing was written")


def commitments_table(okr: Okr) -> tuple[int, int, list[str]]:
    lo, hi = okr.section(COMMITMENTS_HEADING)
    _, header = okr.table(lo, hi)
    return lo, hi, header


def read_commitments(okr: Okr) -> list[dict]:
    """Every row of the register, keyed by canonical column name."""
    lo, hi, header = commitments_table(okr)
    required, optional = commitment_columns()
    at = {c: column_at(header, c) for c in required + optional}
    at["Id"] = commitment_id_at(header)
    out = []
    for i, _ in okr.rows(lo, hi):
        cells = split_row(okr.lines[i])
        row = {"_line": i, "_cells": cells}
        for name, k in at.items():
            row[name] = cells[k] if 0 <= k < len(cells) else ""
        out.append(row)
    return out


#: The column this split replaced. Named once, here, and only ever read — no
#: writer in this file can produce it again.
LEGACY_DUE_COLUMN = "By when"


def legacy_due_at(header: list[str]) -> int:
    """Index of a pre-split `By when` header cell, or -1.

    The **English** spelling only, and that is not an oversight. A Chinese
    register spells the column `截止`, which is `Due`'s own Chinese spelling —
    the two columns are one word in that language, so there is no header cell
    to find and nothing to rename. The split is carried out on the **values**
    instead, which is the half that has an answer in both languages. That is
    the whole lesson of the deleted regex's fifth round, applied to its
    replacement.
    """
    want = {squash(LEGACY_DUE_COLUMN)}
    for i, cell in enumerate(header):
        if squash(cell) in want:
            return i
    return -1


def unsplit_rows(okr: Okr, tracks: list[dict]) -> list[str]:
    """Ids whose `Due` cell is neither empty nor typed — a pre-split register.

    A **typed** question asked of a typed column: is this cell an ISO date or
    an SLA token? It is not the deleted regex under another name, because it
    never
    asks what the prose *means* — every answer of "no" routes the cell to a
    field that is never examined again.
    """
    out = []
    for row in read_commitments(okr):
        try:
            track = track_named(tracks, row.get("Track", ""))
        except Refused:
            track = {}
        if lib.classify_due(track, row.get("Due") or "") not in {
                lib.DUE_UNFILLED, lib.DUE_DATE, lib.DUE_DURATION}:
            out.append((row.get("Id") or "").strip() or f"line {row['_line'] + 1}")
    return out


def migrate_commitments(okr: Okr, tracks: list[dict]) -> dict:
    """Split a pre-TASK-091 `By when` column into `Due` + `By when note`.

    A bare ISO date or SLA token stays where it is and is now typed; anything
    else is moved verbatim into `By when note`, which nothing validates. **No
    cell is dropped and no cell is rewritten** — a moved value is the same
    string in a different column, which is what makes the before/after count
    of non-empty clock cells the proof that nothing was lost.

    ADR-004's *a project migrates once*: this runs from `commit --migrate`,
    and every other write path refuses while the register still needs it
    rather than half-splitting a table on the way past.
    """
    lo, hi, header = commitments_table(okr)
    required, optional = commitment_columns()
    out = {"renamed_column": "", "widened": [], "rows": 0,
           "kept_as_due": 0, "moved_to_note": 0, "empty": 0}

    at_due = column_at(header, "Due")
    at_legacy = legacy_due_at(header)
    if at_due < 0 and at_legacy < 0:
        raise Refused(
            "`## Commitments` has neither a `Due` column nor the `By when` "
            "column it replaced, so there is nothing to split. Nothing was "
            "written")
    if at_due >= 0 and at_legacy >= 0 and at_due != at_legacy:
        # Two clock columns, both populated, is not a migration this tool gets
        # to resolve: for a row carrying a date in each, only the person who
        # made the promise knows which one the party is holding them to. It is
        # also a shape nothing here can produce — a hand edit, or a
        # column-adding migration run before `perry-migrate` learned to stand
        # aside. Reported, and the file left alone.
        raise Refused(
            f"`## Commitments` carries BOTH a `Due` column and the retired "
            f"`{LEGACY_DUE_COLUMN}` column it replaced. A row with a value in "
            f"each is two deadlines for one promise, and only the person who "
            f"made it knows which one the party is holding them to. Move the "
            f"cells you want kept into `Due` or `By when note` and delete the "
            f"`{LEGACY_DUE_COLUMN}` column, then re-run. Nothing was written")
    lang = header_language(header, required + optional)
    if at_due < 0:
        sep, _ = okr.table(lo, hi)
        okr.set_cell(sep - 1, at_legacy, display_name("Due", lang))
        out["renamed_column"] = f"{LEGACY_DUE_COLUMN} → Due"
        lo, hi, header = commitments_table(okr)
        at_due = column_at(header, "Due")

    prose: list[tuple[int, str]] = []
    at_track = column_at(header, "Track")
    at_id = column_at(header, "Id")
    for i in okr.row_indexes(lo, hi):
        out["rows"] += 1
        cells = split_row(okr.lines[i])
        raw = cells[at_due] if 0 <= at_due < len(cells) else ""
        try:
            track = track_named(
                tracks, cells[at_track] if 0 <= at_track < len(cells) else "")
        except Refused:
            track = {}
        classification = lib.classify_due(track, raw)
        if classification == lib.DUE_UNFILLED:
            out["empty"] += 1
        elif classification in {lib.DUE_DATE, lib.DUE_DURATION}:
            out["kept_as_due"] += 1
        elif classification in {lib.DUE_PIPELINE_REQUIRES_DATE,
                              lib.DUE_QUEUE_MISSING_CLOCK}:
            cid = cells[at_id] if 0 <= at_id < len(cells) else "—"
            raise Refused(
                f"commitment {cid!r} has `Due` value {raw!r} that its track "
                f"does not allow ({classification}). Migration will not "
                f"relabel a typed value as prose. Fix the track configuration "
                f"or the value, then retry. Nothing was written")
        else:
            prose.append((i, raw.strip()))

    if prose:
        at_note = column_at(header, "By when note")
        if at_note < 0:
            okr.widen(lo, hi, display_name("By when note", lang))
            out["widened"].append("By when note")
            lo, hi, header = commitments_table(okr)
            at_note = column_at(header, "By when note")
        for i, value in prose:
            cells = split_row(okr.lines[i])
            existing = (cells[at_note] if 0 <= at_note < len(cells) else "").strip()
            okr.set_cell(i, at_note,
                         f"{existing} · {value}" if existing else value)
            okr.set_cell(i, at_due, "")
            out["moved_to_note"] += 1
    return out


def commitment_id_at(header: list[str]) -> int:
    at = column_at(header, "Id")
    if at < 0:
        at = column_at(header, "ID")
    if at < 0:
        raise Refused(
            "`## Commitments` has no `Id` column. Board rows point here by "
            "that id and nothing else, so a register without one cannot be "
            "written to. Nothing was written")
    return at


ID_N_RE = re.compile(r"^(?P<track>.+)/(?P<n>\d+)$")


def mint_commitment_id(track: str, rows: list[dict], events: list[dict],
                       stored_ids: "list[str] | tuple[str, ...]" = ()) -> str:
    """`<track>/<n>`, `<n>` one greater than the highest **for that track**.

    `phases.md`: *"Ids are never reused and never renumbered."* So the search
    is over the table AND the event log — a row created by this tool and later
    deleted from the file by hand is gone from the table and still in the log,
    and reusing its number would silently re-point every board row that carries
    it at a different promise. That is the one failure mode worse than a
    dangling link, because nothing looks broken.

    **And over `okr.jsonl`, which is the only one of the three that holds a row
    this tool never wrote.** TASK-123 measured the hole: a register imported
    with `perry-okr write --from-file` puts its rows in the store and NOT in
    the log — `commit` has never seen them — so deleting `ops/1` from the file
    by hand left the number in the store alone, invisible to both lists above.
    The next `commit --track ops` minted `ops/1` a second time, for a different
    promise, and reported success. `store_drift` did not catch it either: the
    new row's key IS `commitment/ops/1`, so `touches` excused the record it had
    just destroyed as this write's own doing. Reproduced end to end; the store
    is the third list because it is the only one that knew.
    """
    seen = [r.get("Id", "") for r in rows]
    seen += [e.get("id", "") for e in commitment_events(events)]
    seen += list(stored_ids)
    highest = 0
    for cid in seen:
        m = ID_N_RE.match((cid or "").strip().strip("*` "))
        if m and m.group("track") == track:
            highest = max(highest, int(m.group("n")))
    return f"{track}/{highest + 1}"


def find_commitment(rows: list[dict], cid: str, flag: str = "") -> dict:
    """The row `cid` names, or a refusal **naming the flag `cid` arrived on**.

    Three flags reach here — `--id`, `--close`, `--miss` — and all three take
    a value, so a mistyped command puts the NEXT flag in the id's place:
    `commit --id main/1 --close --discharged-by x` refused with
    `'--discharged-by' is not a row`, a sentence that names neither the flag
    that swallowed it nor the one that lost its value.
    """
    for r in rows:
        if r.get("Id", "").strip().strip("*` ") == cid:
            return r
    known = [r.get("Id", "") for r in rows]
    where = f"{flag} " if flag else ""
    raise Refused(f"{where}was given {cid!r}, which is not a row in "
                  f"`OKR.md § Commitments`; present: {known or '(none)'}. "
                  f"Nothing was written")


#: The commitment fields a hand edit can move. `kind` is the record's name and
#: `order` is its position — a row dragged up the table changes `order` and
#: says nothing about any value, which is why `perry-lint § check_store_drift`
#: excludes the same field from the same comparison.
COMMITMENT_FIELDS = tuple(f for f in md_store.STORED["commitment"]
                          if f not in ("kind", "order"))


def register_drift(state_root: Path, project_root: Path, okr) -> dict:
    """How `OKR.md § Commitments` differs from `okr.jsonl`, before any edit.

    TASK-123. **This is the read side of TASK-092's write side**, and until it
    existed `OKR.md` had two writers with opposite notions of which artifact is
    true: `perry-okr` treats `okr.jsonl` as canonical and reports a hand edit,
    while every decision `cmd_commit` makes — a row's `Status`, whether its
    `Due` is in the past, what its `Discharged by` already says, which id is
    free — was read out of the markdown and then written back INTO the store as
    the new truth. Three cases were reproduced end to end before this landed:

      * a hand edit to a row the command touches was absorbed into the store
        and **not reported at all** (`store_drift: []`), because `touches`
        excuses that row as the write's own doing;
      * a hand edit to a row the command does not touch was reported as drift
        and then written into the store anyway — reported AND honoured, which
        is the one outcome ADR-007 exists to prevent;
      * an id whose row was deleted by hand was minted again (see
        `mint_commitment_id`).

    So the store is consulted FIRST, and a cell the two disagree about is a
    hand edit: `cmd_commit` refuses on it rather than deciding against a value
    the canonical file does not carry. Nothing here re-renders `OKR.md` — the
    file is not touched, and once the comparison passes, every cell this tool
    reads out of the markdown is provably the store's own value.

    Presence is reported and never refused, because refusing would buy nothing
    that is not already bought: a record whose row has left is KEPT by
    `write_okr_and_store` and named in `records_the_file_no_longer_renders`,
    its id is protected by `mint_commitment_id`, and `find_commitment` refuses
    by name for a row that is not there. Nothing is lost by proceeding, and
    `phases.md`'s own worked example deletes a row by hand and expects the next
    `commit` to go through.

    A project with no store is not drifted, it **predates the store** — the
    same distinction `logged_status` draws for the log, and for the same
    reason: every register alive today was written by hand, and treating those
    as edits makes the first write on every real project a wall of prompts.
    """
    store = md_store.OKR.store_path(project_root, state_root)
    out = {"store": str(store), "compared": False, "stored_ids": [],
           "disagreements": [], "in_the_store_not_in_the_file": [],
           "in_the_file_not_in_the_store": []}
    if not store.exists():
        return out
    try:
        on_disk = md_store.load_store(store)
    except (OSError, ValueError) as exc:
        raise Refused(
            f"{store} exists but is not readable JSONL "
            f"({type(exc).__name__}: {exc}), so `OKR.md` cannot be compared "
            f"against the file it is a projection of. Move it aside and "
            f"re-run. Nothing was written") from None
    prior, malformed = md_store.validate_records(on_disk)
    if malformed:
        raise Refused(
            f"{store} is malformed at line {malformed[0]['line']} "
            f"({malformed[0]['message']}), so `OKR.md` cannot be compared "
            f"against it. Nothing was written")

    def by_id(records: list[dict]) -> dict:
        return {r.get("id", ""): r for r in records
                if r.get("kind") == "commitment" and r.get("id")}

    stored = by_id(prior)
    live = by_id(md_store.derive(md_store.OKR, okr.text))
    out["compared"] = True
    out["stored_ids"] = sorted(stored)
    for cid in sorted(set(stored) & set(live)):
        for field in COMMITMENT_FIELDS:
            was, now = stored[cid].get(field) or "", live[cid].get(field) or ""
            if was != now:
                out["disagreements"].append(
                    {"id": cid, "field": field, "store": was, "file": now})
    out["in_the_store_not_in_the_file"] = sorted(set(stored) - set(live))
    out["in_the_file_not_in_the_store"] = sorted(set(live) - set(stored))
    return out


def check_register_drift(drift: dict, accept: bool, quiet: bool = False) -> None:
    """Refuse a write decided against a cell `okr.jsonl` does not carry.

    `--accept-hand-edit` is the documented way past it and keeps the meaning it
    already has everywhere else in this tool: **the FILE's value becomes the
    new truth**, and the next write records it. That is DESIGN-005 § 9's
    reconcile — *never silently overwritten, and never silently authoritative
    either* — which is strictly stronger than ADR-007's "reported rather than
    honoured" and satisfies it. `perry-okr diff` asks the same question on its
    own, and `perry-okr render --write` is the other way back.
    """
    if not drift["disagreements"]:
        return
    shown = [f"{d['id']}.{d['field']}: `okr.jsonl` holds "
             f"{d['store'] or '(empty)'!r}, `OKR.md` reads "
             f"{d['file'] or '(empty)'!r}" for d in drift["disagreements"]]
    if accept:
        if not quiet:
            print(f"perry-goals: ⚠ taking the FILE's value on "
                  f"{len(shown)} cell(s) `okr.jsonl` disagrees with, because "
                  f"`--accept-hand-edit` says so:", file=sys.stderr)
            for line in shown[:5]:
                print(f"    {line}", file=sys.stderr)
        return
    raise Refused(
        f"{len(shown)} cell(s) of `OKR.md § Commitments` disagree with "
        f"`okr.jsonl`, which is the canonical record of this register "
        f"(ADR-007 decision 2) — someone edited the row by hand:\n    "
        + "\n    ".join(shown[:5])
        + (f"\n    … and {len(shown) - 5} more" if len(shown) > 5 else "")
        + "\n\n  This write reads those cells to decide what to do, so it "
          "will not run against a value the store does not carry. Re-run with "
          "`--accept-hand-edit` to take `OKR.md`'s values as the truth and "
          "record them, or `perry-okr render --write` to put the file back in "
          "line with the store. Nothing was written")


def check_hand_edit(events: list[dict], row: dict, accept: bool) -> str:
    """The reconcile step DESIGN-005 § 9 requires, in the direction it names.

    The log became canonical for tasks on the user's signature, and the cost
    accepted with it was that a hand edit *"raises a reconcile prompt instead —
    never silently overwritten, and never silently authoritative either"*. So
    the file's value is never replaced by the log's; the disagreement is
    reported, and `--accept-hand-edit` records the FILE's value as the new
    truth. A row the log has never heard of predates the tool and is not an
    edit at all — see `logged_status`.
    """
    expected = logged_status(events, row.get("Id", ""))
    actual = (row.get("Status") or "").strip().strip("*` ")
    if expected is None or squash(expected) == squash(actual):
        return actual
    if not accept:
        raise Refused(
            f"{row.get('Id')} was last written by this tool as "
            f"`{expected}` and `OKR.md` now reads `{actual or '(empty)'}` — "
            f"someone edited the row by hand. That edit is not overwritten and "
            f"not ignored: re-run with `--accept-hand-edit` to take "
            f"`{actual or '(empty)'}` as the truth and record it, or fix the "
            f"row first. Nothing was written")
    return actual


COMMITMENTS_NOTE_FROM_TEMPLATE = "the section is copied from OKR_TEMPLATE.md"


def template_commitments_block() -> list[str]:
    """`goals/state/OKR_TEMPLATE.md § Commitments`, header and note included,
    minus the placeholder row.

    Copied, not restated — `phases.md` says to create the section *from the
    template*, and a second wording of that note in this file would be one more
    place for it to drift out of step with the one users read.
    """
    if not TEMPLATE_PATH.exists():
        raise Refused(f"no template at {TEMPLATE_PATH}; cannot create the "
                      f"`## Commitments` section. Nothing was written")
    doc = Okr(TEMPLATE_PATH)
    lo, hi = doc.section(COMMITMENTS_HEADING)
    sep, _ = doc.table(lo, hi)
    block = [l for l in doc.lines[lo:sep + 1]]
    while block and not block[-1].strip():
        block.pop()
    return block


def cmd_commit(args, ctx) -> dict:
    """Add, update, close or miss one row of `OKR.md § Commitments`."""
    okr, sr = ctx["okr"], ctx["state_root"]
    events = ctx["events"]
    # **The store is consulted before any decision is made against the file.**
    # Every branch below reads `Status`, `Due`, `Discharged by` and the set of
    # ids out of the markdown; `okr.jsonl` is what those fields MEAN (ADR-007
    # decision 2), so a cell the two disagree about is a hand edit and this
    # write does not get to act on it. `--dry-run` is gated too — a plan
    # computed against a drifted file is a plan for a file nobody has.
    drift = register_drift(sr, ctx["project_root"], okr)
    check_register_drift(drift, args.accept_hand_edit, quiet=args.as_json)
    ending = args.close or args.miss
    if args.close and args.miss:
        raise Refused("--close and --miss end a commitment two different ways; "
                      "pass one. Nothing was written")
    if args.migrate and (ending or args.id or args.track or args.due
                         or args.by_when_note or args.promise or args.to):
        raise Refused(
            "--migrate splits the register's clock column and writes nothing "
            "else; it does not take a row's flags. Run it alone, then commit. "
            "Nothing was written")

    if args.migrate:
        if not okr.has_section(COMMITMENTS_HEADING):
            raise Refused(
                "`OKR.md` has no `## Commitments` section, so there is no "
                "register to split. Nothing was written")
        counts = migrate_commitments(okr, ctx["tracks"])
        result = {"migrated": counts, "id": "—", "created_section": False,
                  "widened": list(counts["widened"]), "file": str(okr.path),
                  "register_drift": drift}
        result["event"] = {
            "ts": lib.event_stamp(),
            "event": "commit-migrate", "id": "—", "track": "",
            "actor": args.actor, "from": None, "to": None,
            "counts": result["migrated"]}
        if args.dry_run:
            result["dry_run"] = True
            result["would_write"] = okr.render() != okr.text
            return result
        # `--migrate` rewrites the clock cell of EVERY commitment row, so every
        # commitment key is touched. Nothing else in the file is.
        result.update(write_okr_and_store(
            sr, ctx["project_root"], okr, touched=("commitment/",),
            quiet=args.as_json))
        result["event_written"] = append_event(ctx["project_root"], result["event"])
        return result

    created_section = False
    if not okr.has_section(COMMITMENTS_HEADING):
        if ending or args.id:
            raise Refused(
                "`OKR.md` has no `## Commitments` section, so there is no row "
                "to end. Nothing was written")
        # `phases.md` step 1: refuse if the section is absent and no track is
        # `pipeline` or `queue`. `OKR_TEMPLATE.md` says to omit the section
        # entirely on an all-`project` project, so creating it because someone
        # typed `commit` would add a spine to a shape that has no use for one.
        modes = {(t.get("mode") or "project").lower() for t in ctx["tracks"]}
        if not modes & {"pipeline", "queue"}:
            raise Refused(
                "`OKR.md` has no `## Commitments` section and no track in "
                "`.perry/config.md § Tracks` is `pipeline` or `queue` mode. "
                "That section is the spine for exactly those two modes "
                "(modes/pipeline.md, modes/queue.md); on an all-`project` "
                "project a KR is the commitment, and the party is the project "
                "itself. Nothing was written")
        # Where the template puts it: after Mission / Operating Principles and
        # BEFORE the version blocks. The fallbacks are in that order for a
        # reason a real file made visible — gimegime-pmo has no level-2
        # `## Anti-Goals` (it nests one inside each version) and its
        # `## Versioning` sits at the very bottom, so an Anti-Goals-then-
        # Versioning rule dropped the section between the last version block
        # and the log, which is the one place in that file it does not belong.
        okr.insert_section(
            template_commitments_block(),
            before=[r"Anti-Goals\b|反目标", r"v\d+\s*[::]", r"Versioning|版本记录"])
        created_section = True

    lo, hi, header = commitments_table(okr)
    # ADR-004's *a project migrates once*, at the one point it can be enforced.
    # A register still carrying the pre-split column is not half-fixed on the
    # way past a `--close`: the split is a separate, named, countable write.
    #
    # It speaks only about a table that is OTHERWISE the schema's shape. A
    # register missing `To whom` has cells sitting under the wrong headers
    # entirely, so `Due` reads whatever the shift put there — reporting that
    # as "prose in Due" would name the wrong defect, and the create branch and
    # `perry-lint` already name the right one. `Due` itself is excluded from
    # the test, because a pre-split table is missing it by definition.
    if not created_section:
        misshapen = [c for c in commitment_columns()[0]
                     if c != "Due" and column_at(header, c) < 0]
        stale = [] if misshapen else unsplit_rows(okr, ctx["tracks"])
        if not misshapen and (legacy_due_at(header) >= 0 or stale):
            raise Refused(
                f"`OKR.md § Commitments` predates the `By when` split "
                f"(ADR-007 decision 3): "
                + (f"the table still has a `{LEGACY_DUE_COLUMN}` column"
                   if legacy_due_at(header) >= 0 else
                   f"{len(stale)} row(s) hold prose in `Due` — {stale[:4]}")
                + ". `Due` is typed and `By when note` is prose, and this tool "
                  "will not write into a table that still mixes them. Run "
                  "`perry-goals commit --migrate` first: it moves each cell "
                  "into the field its value belongs to and drops nothing. "
                  "Nothing was written")
    rows = read_commitments(okr)
    required, optional = commitment_columns()
    widened = []

    def ensure(column: str) -> int:
        nonlocal lo, hi, header, rows
        at = column_at(header, column)
        if at >= 0:
            return at
        lang = header_language(header, required + optional)
        header = okr.widen(lo, hi, display_name(column, lang))
        widened.append(column)
        lo, hi = okr.section(COMMITMENTS_HEADING)
        rows = read_commitments(okr)
        return column_at(header, column)

    if ending:
        cid = args.close or args.miss
        row = find_commitment(rows, cid, "--close" if args.close else "--miss")
        was = check_hand_edit(events, row, args.accept_hand_edit)
        to = "closed" if args.close else "missed"
        if squash(was) == to:
            raise Refused(f"{cid} is already `{to}`. Nothing was written")
        if args.miss and not (args.reason or "").strip():
            raise Refused(
                "--miss needs a reason: it is the record of what happened, and "
                "the party the promise was made to is the one person who "
                "cannot see the row. Nothing was written")
        if args.close and not (row.get("Discharged by") or "").strip() \
                and not (args.discharged_by or "").strip():
            raise Refused(
                f"{cid} has an empty `Discharged by`. A promise closed with no "
                f"account of how is indistinguishable from one abandoned "
                f"quietly — pass `--discharged-by <one line>`. Nothing was written")

        okr.set_cell(row["_line"], column_at(header, "Status"), to)
        note = (args.reason if args.miss else args.discharged_by) or ""
        if note.strip():
            at = ensure("Discharged by")
            row = find_commitment(read_commitments(okr), cid)
            existing = (row.get("Discharged by") or "").strip()
            # `Discharged by` is the one cell two different flags write, so
            # the column is not enough to name it — `--miss` fills it from
            # `--reason` and `--close` from `--discharged-by`.
            flag = "--reason" if args.miss else "--discharged-by"
            with naming_the_flag(flag):
                okr.set_cell(row["_line"], at,
                             f"{existing} · {note.strip()}" if existing
                             else note.strip())
        result = {"id": cid, "from": was, "to": to}
        event = {"ts": lib.event_stamp(),
                 "event": "commit-close" if args.close else "commit-miss",
                 "id": cid, "track": row.get("Track", ""),
                 "promise": row.get("Promise", ""),
                 "note": note.strip(), "actor": args.actor,
                 "from": was, "to": to}

    elif args.id:
        row = find_commitment(rows, args.id, "--id")
        was = check_hand_edit(events, row, args.accept_hand_edit)
        changes = {}
        if args.due is not None:
            # `phases.md`: *"A missed commitment is recorded, never silently
            # re-dated."* Editing `Due` on a promise whose date has passed
            # erases the fact that it was missed, and the party it was made to
            # is the one person who cannot see the edit.
            current = real_date(row.get("Due", ""))
            if current and current < date.today() and squash(was) == "active":
                raise Refused(
                    f"{args.id}'s `Due` is {current:%Y-%m-%d}, which is in "
                    f"the past, and its `Status` is `active` — that promise was "
                    f"missed. Re-dating it here would erase the fact, and the "
                    f"party it was made to is the one person who cannot see the "
                    f"edit. Use `--miss {args.id} --reason <what happened>`, "
                    f"then `commit` a new row under the new date. "
                    f"Nothing was written")
            check_due(track_named(ctx["tracks"], row.get("Track", "")), args.due)
            changes["Due"] = args.due
        # **`By when note` is prose and this is the whole of its handling.**
        # No format check, no vocabulary, no regex — ADR-007 rule 2. It is not
        # swept into the whitespace guard below either: clearing the note is
        # how a wrong one is withdrawn, and `Due` still carries the promise.
        if args.by_when_note is not None:
            changes["By when note"] = args.by_when_note
        # **Whitespace-only is a refusal on create and was an ERASE on amend.**
        # `--promise '   '` was rejected outright by the create branch and
        # silently wiped the cell here, because this path tested `is not None`
        # where the other tests `.strip()`. The user's promise text was gone,
        # the event recorded the spaces, and nothing said so. Same tool, same
        # value, two answers — the defect this whole task is about, one flag
        # further along.
        #
        # `--discharged-by ''` is deliberately NOT swept in: clearing it is how
        # a mistaken discharge is undone, and there is no other way to say
        # "this is not discharged after all".
        for _flag, _column in (("promise", "Promise"), ("to", "To whom")):
            _v = getattr(args, _flag)
            if _v is not None and not _v.strip():
                raise Refused(
                    f"--{_flag} was given only whitespace, which would erase "
                    f"the {_column} cell rather than change it. Pass the new "
                    f"text, or use --close / --miss to retire the row. "
                    f"Nothing was written")
        if args.promise is not None:
            changes["Promise"] = args.promise
        if args.to is not None:
            changes["To whom"] = args.to
        if args.discharged_by is not None:
            changes["Discharged by"] = args.discharged_by
        if not changes:
            raise Refused(
                f"--id {args.id} with nothing to change. Pass --by / --promise "
                f"/ --to / --discharged-by, or use --close / --miss. "
                f"Nothing was written")
        for column, value in changes.items():
            at = ensure(column)
            line = find_commitment(read_commitments(okr), args.id)["_line"]
            with naming_the_flag(FLAG_FOR_COLUMN.get(column, "")):
                okr.set_cell(line, at, value)
        result = {"id": args.id, "changed": sorted(changes)}
        event = {"ts": lib.event_stamp(),
                 "event": "commit-update", "id": args.id,
                 "track": row.get("Track", ""), "changed": changes,
                 "actor": args.actor, "from": was, "to": was}

    else:
        if not (args.track or "").strip():
            raise Refused("--track is required: a commitment is filed against a "
                          "track, and the track's mode decides what `Due` "
                          "may say. Nothing was written")
        if not (args.promise or "").strip():
            raise Refused("--promise is required. Nothing was written")
        if not (args.to or "").strip():
            # `phases.md` step 3, and it is a routing rule, not a validation
            # one: the tool says where the thing belongs instead.
            raise Refused(
                "--to is required and has no default. A promise with no named "
                "party is a KR, and belongs under an Objective in this file "
                "rather than in the commitments register. Nothing was written")
        track = track_named(ctx["tracks"], args.track, "--track")
        check_due(track, args.due or "")
        cid = mint_commitment_id(args.track, rows, events,
                                 drift["stored_ids"])
        values = {"Id": cid, "Track": args.track, "Promise": args.promise,
                  "To whom": args.to, "Due": args.due,
                  # Prose, written exactly as given and never inspected.
                  "By when note": args.by_when_note or "",
                  # `phases.md` step 5: `Status: active`, `Discharged by` empty.
                  # That cell is free prose describing HOW the promise gets
                  # satisfied; it is never a list of row ids.
                  "Status": "active",
                  "Discharged by": args.discharged_by or ""}
        missing = [c for c in required if column_at(header, c) < 0]
        if missing:
            raise Refused(
                f"`## Commitments` is missing the column(s) {missing}, which "
                f"`schema/state-schema.json` declares required. Fix the table's "
                f"header first — widening it here would pad every row of a "
                f"register this tool did not write. Nothing was written")
        if values["Discharged by"]:
            ensure("Discharged by")
        if values["By when note"]:
            ensure("By when note")
        order = [canonical_of(h, required + optional) for h in header]
        # A row is written in one call, so which flag is named depends on
        # which CELL `render_row` refused — `exc.index` is a position in this
        # list, and this list is the only place that position has a meaning.
        with naming_the_flag([FLAG_FOR_COLUMN.get(c, "") for c in order]):
            _, line = okr.append_table_row(
                lo, hi, [values.get(c, "") for c in order])
        result = {"id": cid, "row": line, "track": args.track}
        event = {"ts": lib.event_stamp(),
                 "event": "commit", "id": cid, "track": args.track,
                 "mode": (track.get("mode") or "project").lower(),
                 "promise": args.promise, "to_whom": args.to,
                 "due": args.due, "by_when_note": args.by_when_note or "",
                 "actor": args.actor, "from": None, "to": "active"}

    result["created_section"] = created_section
    result["widened"] = widened
    result["file"] = str(okr.path)
    result["register_drift"] = drift
    result["event"] = event
    if args.dry_run:
        result["dry_run"] = True
        result["would_write"] = okr.render() != okr.text
        return result
    # The one row this command wrote. A widened table pads every OTHER row of
    # the register too, so `widened` opens the whole kind — a padding change is
    # this write's doing, not a hand edit somebody made.
    touched = ("commitment/",) if widened or created_section \
        else (f"commitment/{result['id']}",)
    result.update(write_okr_and_store(
        sr, ctx["project_root"], okr, touched=touched,
        quiet=args.as_json))
    result["event_written"] = append_event(ctx["project_root"], event)
    return result


def canonical_of(header_cell: str, names: list[str]) -> str:
    """Which declared column this header cell is, or the cell itself.

    Table-local, for the reason `column_spellings` gives. A cell the schema
    does not declare keeps its own name so a project's extra column is filled
    from `values` if the caller named it and left empty otherwise — never
    dropped, and never shifted into by position.
    """
    for name in names:
        if squash(header_cell) in set(column_spellings(name)):
            return name
    return squash(header_cell)


COMMANDS = {"list": None, "commit": cmd_commit, "link": cmd_link}


class Args:
    pass


def parse(argv: list[str]) -> Args:
    a = Args()
    a.cmd = a.root = a.level = None
    a.id = a.track = a.promise = a.to = a.due = a.by_when_note = None
    a.close = a.miss = a.reason = a.discharged_by = None
    a.actor = "agent"
    a.as_json = a.dry_run = a.accept_hand_edit = a.migrate = False
    #: `link`'s three alternative shapes, and its positional arguments. They
    #: are collected rather than consumed by name because
    #: `goals/reference/linkage.md` writes the grammar that way — `--alias
    #: <PROJECT-ID> "<name>"` is two values after a mode flag — and a page
    #: documenting a command that takes its arguments differently is the stale
    #: instruction `tests/test_procedures_call_the_tool.py` exists to end.
    a.alias = a.unlinked = a.project = False
    a.rest = []
    flags = {"--root": "root", "--level": "level", "--id": "id",
             "--track": "track", "--promise": "promise", "--to": "to",
             "--due": "due", "--by-when-note": "by_when_note",
             "--close": "close", "--miss": "miss",
             "--reason": "reason", "--discharged-by": "discharged_by",
             "--actor": "actor"}
    i = 0
    while i < len(argv):
        t = argv[i]
        if t in ("-h", "--help"):
            print(__doc__.strip())
            sys.exit(0)
        elif t == "--json":
            a.as_json = True
        elif t == "--dry-run":
            a.dry_run = True
        elif t == "--migrate":
            a.migrate = True
        elif t in ("--alias", "--unlinked", "--project"):
            setattr(a, t[2:], True)
        elif t == "--accept-hand-edit":
            a.accept_hand_edit = True
        elif t == "--by":
            # **The flag the split removed, refused by name rather than
            # swallowed.** `--by` fed one column that held both a date and
            # prose; silently aliasing it to `--due` would refuse every
            # standing commitment whose deadline was worded rather than dated,
            # with a message about a flag the caller never typed.
            raise Refused(
                "--by is gone. `By when` was one column carrying two value "
                "spaces, and it is now two fields: `--due` takes an ISO date "
                "or an SLA token (3d, 2w) and is validated, `--by-when-note` "
                "takes the words the deadline was promised in and is not. "
                "Nothing was written")
        elif t in flags:
            i += 1
            setattr(a, flags[t], argv[i] if i < len(argv) else None)
        elif a.cmd is None:
            a.cmd = t
        else:
            # Before `link` there was nowhere for these to go and they were
            # dropped in silence — `commit --track ops extra` ignored `extra`.
            # They are collected now, and every subcommand that takes none
            # refuses when the list is not empty.
            a.rest.append(t)
        i += 1
    return a


#: Printed after a `commit` write. The board-side link — a row's `Commitment`
#: cell carrying an `Id` from this table — is the `work` lane's write, and this
#: tool asks in chat rather than reaching across (`SKILL.md § The hand-off
#: contract`). The link runs from the board side on purpose, so this register
#: never accumulates ids that rot as rows close.
def handoff_line(cid: str) -> str:
    return (f"  ↪ hand-off: to point work at this promise, `/perry work` puts "
            f"`{cid}` in a board row's `Commitment` cell. `goals` does not "
            f"write BOARD.md.")


def main(argv: list[str]) -> int:
    try:
        args = parse(argv)
    except Refused as exc:
        # A refusal raised while reading the flags has no `args` to ask about
        # `--json`, so the question is put to `argv` — the same answer, one
        # layer earlier. Without this the retired `--by` would have exited
        # through the module-level handler with empty stdout under `--json`,
        # which is the shape of defect `UnrenderableCell`'s handler already
        # cost this file once.
        if "--json" in argv:
            print(json.dumps({"refused": str(exc)}, ensure_ascii=False, indent=2))
        else:
            print(f"perry-goals: refused — {exc}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 1
    if args.cmd not in COMMANDS:
        print(f"perry-goals: expected one of {' / '.join(COMMANDS)} "
              f"(try --help)", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    try:
        if args.level and args.level not in ("overall", "phase"):
            raise Refused(f"--level {args.level!r} is not `overall` or `phase`")
        project_root = (Path(args.root).expanduser().resolve() if args.root
                        else Path(os.environ.get("PERRY_PROJECT") or Path.cwd()).resolve())
        state_root = P.resolve_state_root(project_root)
        if args.cmd == "list":
            result = build(project_root, state_root, args.level)
        else:
            # ADR-004, before the lock and before the command runs: a refusal
            # must mean nothing was written, and a dry run must preview the
            # refusal rather than the write it would not be allowed to perform.
            # **The gate is about the file this command writes**, which is
            # not always `OKR.md` any more: `link` writes the phase's linkage
            # register. ADR-004 § 5 is one key, one file — passing the gated
            # name of a file the command does not touch would refuse a write
            # for the shape of something unrelated, and pass one for a file
            # nobody checked.
            gate_file = GATED_FILE
            register_file = None
            if args.cmd == "link":
                register_file = register_path(state_root)
                gate_file = register_file.relative_to(state_root).as_posix()
            gate = perry_conform().gate(project_root, state_root, gate_file,
                                        tool="perry-goals", root_arg=args.root)
            # **`--migrate` is exempt, and the exemption is the whole point of
            # the gate rather than a hole in it.** The gate stops writers
            # writing into a file that is not Perry's shape (ADR-004); a
            # pre-split register IS out of shape, by exactly the defect this
            # command exists to fix, so enforcing it here would make the file
            # permanently unmigratable — refuse to write, refuse to fix, no
            # third command. `perry-migrate` is exempt from its own gate for
            # the same reason, and this is the transform it hands over.
            # Three outcomes, and they are mutually exclusive — the first
            # version wrote them as three independent `if`s, which was
            # unreachable while the gate shipped advisory (`gate.ok` was always
            # true) and started double-printing the moment TASK-047 flipped the
            # default: an exempt `--migrate` run announced the exemption and
            # then announced itself as advisory, under `enforce`.
            if not gate.ok:
                if not args.migrate:
                    raise Refused(gate.message)
                if not args.as_json:
                    print(f"perry-goals: ⚠ migrating a file the conformance "
                          f"gate refuses — that is what a migration is. "
                          f"{gate.message}", file=sys.stderr)
            elif gate.message and not args.as_json:
                print(f"perry-goals: ⚠ conformance ({gate.mode}) — "
                      f"{gate.message}", file=sys.stderr)
            with project_lock(state_root):
                ctx = {"project_root": project_root, "state_root": state_root}
                if register_file is not None:
                    # `link` needs no `OKR.md` and no track register: it writes
                    # one file and reads one file, and requiring the other two
                    # would refuse an attribution on a project whose OKR is
                    # mid-interview.
                    ctx["register"] = Register(register_file)
                else:
                    ctx.update({"okr": Okr(state_root / "OKR.md"),
                                "tracks": tracks_of(project_root),
                                "events": read_events(project_root)})
                result = COMMANDS[args.cmd](args, ctx)
            # `list` is a published contract and gets nothing added to it.
            # Every other payload is Perry-internal, so the verdict rides along
            # there rather than making an agent shell out to a second tool to
            # learn its write was allowed under protest.
            result["conformance"] = gate.as_dict()
    # A value a markdown table row cannot carry, translated into the ordinary
    # refusal channel **here, where `args` is in scope** — the twin in
    # `bin/perry-task § main` is in the same place for the same reason. The
    # previous version of this handler sat at module level, which is a scope
    # with no `args` and therefore no way to answer `--json`: every line-break
    # refusal exited 1 with an empty stdout for a caller reading the payload.
    # `render_row` raises before the write, so a refusal still means nothing
    # was written.
    except UnrenderableCell as exc:
        msg = unrenderable_refusal(exc)
        if args.as_json:
            print(json.dumps({"refused": msg}, ensure_ascii=False, indent=2))
        else:
            print(f"perry-goals: refused — {msg}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 1
    except Refused as exc:
        if args.as_json:
            print(json.dumps({"refused": str(exc)}, ensure_ascii=False, indent=2))
        else:
            print(f"perry-goals: refused — {exc}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 1

    if args.as_json:
        print(json.dumps(result, ensure_ascii=False, indent=2))
    elif args.cmd == "commit" and result.get("migrated"):
        m = result["migrated"]
        verb = "would split" if args.dry_run else "split"
        print(f"perry-goals: {verb} OKR.md § Commitments — {m['rows']} row(s): "
              f"{m['kept_as_due']} kept as `Due`, {m['moved_to_note']} moved to "
              f"`By when note`, {m['empty']} already empty")
        if m["renamed_column"]:
            print(f"  · header cell renamed: {m['renamed_column']}")
        for column in m["widened"]:
            print(f"  · widened the table with a `{column}` column; every row "
                  f"was padded for it")
        print(f"  · {m['kept_as_due'] + m['moved_to_note']} non-empty clock "
              f"cell(s) before, {m['kept_as_due'] + m['moved_to_note']} after "
              f"— nothing was dropped")
    elif args.cmd == "link":
        if result.get("already"):
            print(f"perry-goals: nothing to write — {result['already']}")
        else:
            verb = "would write" if args.dry_run else "wrote"
            what = {
                "edge": lambda r: f"{r.get('task')} → {r.get('kr')}",
                "unlinked": lambda r: f"{r.get('task')} declared unlinked",
                "alias": lambda r: f"{r.get('project')} also answers to "
                                   f"{r.get('alias')!r}",
                "project": lambda r: f"{r.get('project')} serves "
                                     f"{r.get('serves')} ({r.get('name')!r})",
            }[result["mode"]](result)
            print(f"perry-goals: {verb} {Path(result['file']).name} — {what}")
            if result.get("undeclared_unlinked"):
                print("  · removed from `unlinked[]` in the same edit — it "
                      "would otherwise render as attributed and drifting")
            for line in result["diff"]:
                print(f"  {line}")
            print(f"  ↪ validate: \"$PERRY_HOME/bin/perry-lint\" --root .")
    elif args.cmd == "commit":
        verb = "would write" if args.dry_run else "wrote"
        print(f"perry-goals: {verb} OKR.md § Commitments — {result['id']}"
              f"{' → ' + result['to'] if result.get('to') else ''}")
        if result.get("created_section"):
            print("  · created the `## Commitments` section from "
                  "goals/state/OKR_TEMPLATE.md — header and note included")
        for column in result.get("widened") or []:
            print(f"  · widened the table with a `{column}` column; every row "
                  f"was padded for it")
        print(handoff_line(result["id"]))
    else:
        o = result["okr"]
        print(f"  OKR {o['version'] or '—'} · {o['mission'][:60] or '—'}")
        ph = result["phase"]
        if ph:
            print(f"  phase #{ph['number']} {ph['slug']} · day {ph['day']}")
        for k in result["krs"]:
            n = ("—" if k["target"] is None or k["current"] is None
                 else f"{k['current']}/{k['target']}")
            # The pair is never printed bare any more. `asserted` says the
            # number was typed by an author and not measured by anything, and
            # the tally beside it is a count of TASKS — the two are printed
            # apart, in different units, so neither can be read as the other.
            t = k["linked_task_completion"]
            note = "" if k["current_provenance"]["state"] == "unasserted" else (
                "asserted" if not k["current_staleness"]["stale"] else "STALE")
            edge = (f"  {t['done'] + t['dropped']}/{t['total']} tasks closed"
                    if t["total"] else "")
            print(f"  {k['id']:10} {k['level']:8} {n:>9} {note:<8}"
                  f"{edge:<22}{k['title'][:44]}")
        c = result["conformance"]
        print(f"\n  {result['counts']['krs']} KR(s) · "
              f"{len(c['krs_without_numbers'])} with no target/current pair")
        print("  no `current` here is a measurement — every one is asserted by "
              "an author; the task counts are not progress")
        if c["krs_with_stale_current"]:
            print(f"  ⚠ {len(c['krs_with_stale_current'])} KR(s) whose linked "
                  f"tasks moved since the number was asserted: "
                  + ", ".join(c["krs_with_stale_current"]))
        if not c["linkage_present"]:
            print("  ⚠ no linkage register — no KR carries task edges or a target")
    return 0


if __name__ == "__main__":
    try:
        sys.exit(main(sys.argv[1:]))
    # See the twin in `bin/perry-task § main`. Both writers call `render_row`,
    # so both need the translation; a guard installed in only the tool where
    # the bug happened to surface is the mistake `render_row`'s own docstring
    # is about.
    except UnrenderableCell as exc:
        # **The last resort, not the handler.** `main` catches this where
        # `args` is in scope; what is left for module level is whatever could
        # be raised outside that `try` — today, nothing. It stays because the
        # alternative to a refusal here is a traceback, which is what this
        # file printed for 88 of 88 measured line-break refusals when the
        # ONLY handler lived here: it was a verbatim copy of `perry-task`'s
        # block, which runs inside `main()` where `args` is a local, so every
        # one of them ended in `NameError: name 'args' is not defined` and
        # `--json` got empty stdout. A test asserted `"line break" in stderr`
        # and passed **on the traceback**, whose own last line carries the
        # phrase. That is why nothing caught it.
        #
        # The flag is carried on the exception, set by whoever raises it —
        # the only layer that both knows the flag and is in scope. See
        # `naming_the_flag`.
        print(f"perry-goals: refused — {unrenderable_refusal(exc)}",
              file=sys.stderr)
        sys.exit(1)
    except Refused as exc:
        print(f"perry-goals: refused — {exc}", file=sys.stderr)
        sys.exit(1)
